{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat draft_reference_gaps.md; printf '\\\\n--- GAP JSON ---\\\\n'; cat draft_reference_gaps.json\"","aggregated_output":"# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n\n**Status:** ready\n\nOne medium-priority benchmark-source citation gap remains for the primary Open LLM six-task panel; the existing related-work and results coverage does not justify broader retrieval.\n\n## Review Basis\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, the 25 entries in `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json`, the 57 claim mappings in `claims_grounding.json`, the grounding-source and final-evaluation records in `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. The memory summary supplies qualitative context only. Existing reference provenance limits substantive reading to canonical metadata and abstracts; this memo does not infer stronger support from those records.\n\n## Target: method-open-llm-panel-source\n\n- **Section:** Method, opening paragraph of “Public score panels and experimental protocol” (`paper.tex:97`).\n- **Priority:** medium.\n- **Citation intent:** benchmark_context.\n- **Anchor:** “the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates”.\n- **Missing literature:** Official Open LLM leaderboard six-task suite and raw-score documentation.\n- **Reason:** The paragraph names a primary score-panel source without directly citing its official leaderboard or suite documentation. Related Work cites IFEval, BBH, MATH, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro individually, but those sources do not attribute their assembly into this leaderboard panel. No corresponding suite-level source appears among the existing bibliography or provenance entries. The supplementary revision hashes and local acquisition manifest already ground the actual inputs; this is a reader-facing source-attribution gap, not a missing-data or unsupported-results problem.\n- **Focused search topic:** Official Open LLM leaderboard documentation for the six-task suite IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro, including raw-score fields.\n- **Minimal later change:** Add one verified canonical official source at this paragraph. If available documentation describes a different leaderboard generation or standardized rather than raw scores, do not treat it as support for the pinned panel. Retain the recorded revisions and normalization details as the authority for the actual analyzed inputs. No method or results rewrite is needed.\n\n## Areas Not Flagged\n\n- Related Work directly cites the benchmark families, social-choice aggregation, robust estimation, leakage and contamination, validity, and uncertainty literature it discusses. No uncited named comparator family there warrants an additional retrieval target.\n- Introduction motivation can use the existing benchmark-composition, social-choice, and contamination references if local citation placement is desired; this does not require new references. The discussion of median and mean behavior is explained by the paper's own definitions rather than asserted as an external finding.\n- Exact enumeration, sorted-headroom optimality, monotonicity, and feasible-support bounds are explained and tied to implementation evidence. The paper disclaims optimizer novelty; adding generic optimization citations would not close a demonstrated gap.\n- Quantitative results, cohort definitions, and certificate scope are grounded in the recorded evaluation and claim mappings, not candidates for bibliographic repair. Real training costs and response calibration remain explicit limitations, not invitations to expand the story.\n\n## Retrieval Boundary\n\nThis memo requests only the single source-attribution lookup above, not a citation-count increase or a broader literature pass. Any later lookup must respect the recorded strategy's prohibited-study and no-citation-graph-expansion restrictions. No reference retrieval was performed, and `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `references_provenance.json` were not modified.\n\n--- GAP JSON ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"summary\": \"One medium-priority benchmark-source citation gap remains for the primary Open LLM six-task panel; the existing related-work and results coverage does not justify broader retrieval.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [\n    \"Official Open LLM leaderboard six-task suite and raw-score documentation\"\n  ],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"method-open-llm-panel-source\",\n      \"section\": \"Method\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"reason\": \"The opening paragraph of Public score panels and experimental protocol identifies the primary Open LLM six-task contents snapshot without a direct citation to its leaderboard or suite documentation. Related Work cites all six constituent benchmarks, but neither the bibliography nor its provenance includes the source that assembles this panel. Supplementary revision hashes and the local acquisition manifest already ground the actual inputs, so this is a reader-facing source-attribution gap, not missing empirical evidence. One canonical official source documenting this suite and its raw-score fields would close it without changing the analysis.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"Official Open LLM leaderboard documentation for the six-task suite IFEval BBH MATH level 5 GPQA MuSR MMLU-Pro and raw-score fields\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*reference*plan*' -g '*SKILL.md'; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\\\\n'; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; printf '\\\\n--- PLAN SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\\\\n'; cat reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- DRAFT ---\\\\n'; sed -n '1,210p' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- SUMMARY ---\\\\n'; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*reference*plan*' -g '*SKILL.md'; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\\\\n'; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; printf '\\\\n--- PLAN SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\\\\n'; cat reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- DRAFT ---\\\\n'; sed -n '1,210p' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- SUMMARY ---\\\\n'; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"/workspace/paper\nreference_query_plan.md\nreference_query_plan.json\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\nreference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261117_131854.log\nreference_query_plan_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261117_131854.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan.json\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n\n--- PLAN SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Conditional task-support investment needed for a fixed target model to become strictly first on public multi-task score matrices under mean, median, and trimmed-mean aggregation, with bounded score uplifts and explicit attainment certificates.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Fixed-rival score-space intervention: selected coordinates change from x_j to x_j + alpha*(1-x_j), with alpha in {0.25, 0.5, 1}; investment is selected-task cardinality or its panel-normalized fraction, not measured training cost\",\n    \"Equal-weight arithmetic mean, conventional median averaging the two middle values for even panels, and symmetric trimmed mean dropping floor(0.2*m) scores from each end\",\n    \"Exact sorted-headroom support selection for the mean and exhaustive support enumeration for panels of at most six tasks\",\n    \"Deterministic marginal-aggregate greedy support selection for larger median and trimmed-mean panels, verified against fixed rivals with strict-first tolerance 1e-10\",\n    \"Conservative lower bounds and feasible support witnesses distinguishing optimal, bounded/unresolved, and infeasible decisions\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Robust statistics and finite-sample sensitivity of medians and trimmed means; estimator robustness is not automatically leaderboard manipulation resistance\",\n    \"Social-choice approaches to multi-task agent evaluation; ordinal aggregation is related work, not an implemented comparator\",\n    \"Strategic post-training incentives; contrast multi-developer strategic models with this run's single-target, fixed-rival counterfactual\",\n    \"Task-selection sensitivity and the benchmark lottery\",\n    \"Evaluation-data contamination as motivation and a limitation of interpreting assumed score uplifts causally\",\n    \"Executed no-intervention, random-order, and marginal-greedy support baselines; no separate generic optimization literature search is needed for these elementary controls\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"HELM MMLU release v1.13.0: 57 subject-score coordinates\",\n    \"Open LLM Leaderboard contents snapshot 9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2: six raw-score coordinates, IFEval, BBH, MATH Lvl 5, GPQA, MUSR, MMLU-PRO\",\n    \"Open LLM Leaderboard results snapshot aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5: separately reported complete 24-subtask BBH panels with exact model-revision matches; not pooled with primary six-task contents scores\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"Conditional attainability under response ceilings: final evaluation reports both attainment shortfalls and attained targets; alpha is an assumed score-response parameter, not a measured training response\",\n    \"Exact small-panel certification versus nonzero aggregate certificate gaps on the evaluated workload; a feasible greedy witness need not establish minimum support\",\n    \"Task granularity and panel selection delimit interpretation across subject-level MMLU, six-task contents scores, and separate BBH sensitivity panels; this is not architecture transfer\",\n    \"No qualifying search winner was found and the baseline was selected; proposed rank-window and coupled-clipped variants do not support claims of methodological improvement\",\n    \"No evidence establishes a universal robustness ordering among the three aggregation rules or an empirical cost of actual contamination\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"median trimmed mean finite-sample replacement breakdown order statistics\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Find foundational robust-statistics treatment of the actual order-statistic aggregators and sensitivity to changing a subset of coordinates. Use only independently screened foundational sources, not leaderboard-specific discovery. Breakdown is a conceptual comparator, not the same estimand as bounded one-sided support cost.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory\\\" 2312.03121\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference positions aggregation of multi-task evaluations within social choice. Contrast ordinal/rank aggregation with the run's cardinal mean, median, and trimmed mean without implying that Borda was tested. Resolve only the canonical supplied record.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Leaderboard Incentives: Model Rankings under Strategic Post-Training\\\" 2603.08371\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference supplies the nearest named incentive-model comparator. Check its actual assumptions later, distinguishing strategic interaction among developers from this run's fixed rivals and exogenous score-uplift ceilings. No equilibrium or training effect is inferred here.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"The Benchmark Lottery\\\" 2107.07002\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference contextualizes task-panel sensitivity. The workload contains full and six-coordinate panels plus a separate BBH sensitivity panel; task selection and aggregation should not be conflated, and no rule-order reversal is claimed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding\\\" 2009.03300\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Ground the 57-subject MMLU task structure used by the HELM score matrix. The benchmark paper does not by itself establish the provenance or settings of the HELM v1.13.0 snapshot.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Holistic Evaluation of Language Models\\\" 2211.09110\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Ground the evaluation framework supplying the HELM public results. Pair the canonical paper with already-recorded v1.13.0 source metadata; do not assume identical evaluation settings across all model rows.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them\\\" 2210.09261\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Identify BBH as the source of the separately reported 24-subtask score panel. The paper title is benchmark context only: this run does not compare chain-of-thought prompting or architectures, and it does not justify treating contents and results runs as interchangeable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"open-llm-leaderboard/contents\\\" \\\"9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2\\\"\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Resolve citation metadata directly from the official pinned dataset record for the six raw-score coordinates. Use the supplied canonical Hugging Face dataset revision rather than broad search if needed; retain the separate results revision aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5 for BBH provenance. Do not invent an associated paper or data license.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater\\\" 2311.01964\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference motivates evaluation-data exposure while marking the key causal gap: the run manipulates published score coordinates and neither trains models nor observes contamination-induced improvements. Its findings must be read before any substantive citation is written.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any attempt to identify or discover the prohibited prior study by title, authors, results, or paraphrases of the research question, including broad searches combining targeted investment, leaderboard manipulation, and aggregation rules\",\n    \"Citation-graph expansion, cited-by searches, related-paper recommendations, surveys, discussion pages, mirrors, code repositories, or social posts that could expose the prohibited study; do not inspect reference lists to discover neighbors\",\n    \"Broad strategic leaderboard or benchmark-cheating discovery searches; use only exact supplied starter identifiers and primary dataset records for those topics\",\n    \"Searches for Certified Attainability Frontiers, Amortized Rank Window Certificates, or Coupled Clipped Score Certificates as if these run-local proposal labels were established literature or successful methods\",\n    \"Queries claiming tested Borda, game-theoretic equilibria, adversarial training, training-cost curves, observed contamination effects, or new optimization bounds absent from the selected-method evidence\",\n    \"Architecture, representation-learning, or chain-of-thought transition queries; no controlled architecture or prompting transition was evaluated\",\n    \"Queries presupposing universal median robustness, an aggregation-order reversal, cross-benchmark transfer, or superiority of an unqualified candidate\",\n    \"Generic optimization, generic benchmarking, scientific method, paper-writing, citation-volume, or campaign-chronology queries\",\n    \"Unpinned latest leaderboard searches, Arena-specific detours, or standalone searches for every component benchmark without a corresponding scientific comparison\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n\n--- DRAFT ---\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption,longtable}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Response Ceilings and Targeted Investment\\\\in Multitask Leaderboards}\n\\author{}\n\\newcommand{\\cost}{K}\n\\newcommand{\\eps}{\\varepsilon}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nMultitask leaderboards compress heterogeneous scores into a single ranking, creating incentives to improve selected tasks. Does replacing the mean with a median make attaining first place more expensive? We study this question under a transparent counterfactual: only a target model changes, each selected score closes a specified fraction of its remaining headroom, and investment counts selected task coordinates. Public HELM and Open LLM score panels permit comparisons of mean, conventional median, and symmetric trimmed mean. On fixed, jointly attainable cohorts in the six-task panel, exact minimum-support comparisons reverse between half and full headroom: median-minus-mean support fractions range from $-0.130$ to $-0.069$ at half headroom and from $+0.127$ to $+0.157$ at full headroom across sampling seeds. Attainability also differs substantially across rules. Larger subject panels yield exact mean costs but only bounded costs for order-statistic rules. Thus aggregation resistance is conditional on response ceilings, target selection, and certificate strength. These are score-space sensitivity results, not measured training costs or evidence of real contamination effects.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nMultitask benchmarks make model comparison manageable by replacing a vector of task scores with an overall ranking. Their breadth is valuable: knowledge, instruction following, and reasoning need not improve together. But this compression also defines incentives. A developer interested in one model's position may concentrate effort on selected benchmark components rather than improving uniformly. The resulting question is not merely whether rankings change when a task is added or removed. It is how much targeted change to an existing score vector is sufficient to move a fixed target above every rival.\n\nIt is tempting to regard an order-statistic aggregator as intrinsically more resistant to concentrated investment than an arithmetic mean. The mean responds to every coordinate, whereas the median depends on central values and a trimmed mean discards tails. This intuition does not by itself answer the ranking question. Raising a weak score can have substantial effect on the mean but none on the median until the score crosses a central rank. Conversely, a median threshold may be reachable by modifying a relatively small set of central coordinates, while a mean threshold still requires improvement spread across many tasks. The permitted magnitude of each change is therefore part of the question, not an incidental nuisance parameter.\n\nWe formalize investment as the number of task coordinates receiving a bounded target-only uplift. The model is deliberately agnostic about the training intervention that might produce it. It asks a reproducible question about public score geometry with explicit ceilings and fixed competitors. We compare arithmetic mean, conventional median, and symmetric trimmed mean on public HELM subject scores and Open LLM task scores. A separate BBH subtask sensitivity check is kept distinct from the primary panels. These are different resolutions of evaluation, not interchangeable units of real-world training expense.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L16-L28]]\n\nOur central observation is a conditional ordering reversal. Holding the target cohort fixed within each sampling seed, the median requires less support on average than the mean at half headroom, but more at full headroom. This comparison uses exact costs on the six-task panel. It is not inferred from a mixture of successful and unsuccessful targets, or from heuristic solutions on larger panels. Alongside this contrast, we report all-target attainability, support intervals where exactness is unavailable, and matched selection-policy comparisons.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\nThe contributions are an explicit, implementable investment definition; an exact matched-cohort demonstration of response-dependent aggregation ordering; and an evidence-strength-aware comparison that separates attainability, minimum costs, and feasible upper bounds. We do not propose a new optimizer or infer an isolated component effect from uncontrolled changes. The main implication is methodological: an aggregation rule should not be described as resistant to targeted investment without specifying the response model and which cost statements are actually certified.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/investment_overview.png}\n\\caption{The studied counterfactual: modify only selected target coordinates, hold rivals fixed, and compare aggregation conditions. The outcome is an exact minimum support, certified unattainability, or a lower--upper support interval. No model training is represented.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Evaluation breadth and benchmark composition.}\nMMLU evaluates knowledge across subjects \\citep{MMLU}; HELM emphasizes broad, transparent evaluation across scenarios and metrics \\citep{HELM}. BIG-bench and BBH characterize diverse tasks and difficult reasoning settings \\citep{BIGBench,BBH}. The six-task panel studied here combines instruction following \\citep{IFEval}, challenging mathematics \\citep{MATH}, expert question answering \\citep{GPQA}, narrative reasoning \\citep{MuSR}, and knowledge-oriented reasoning \\citep{MMLUPro}, alongside BBH. These source works explain the tasks' purposes; our empirical claims concern the pinned score snapshots, not a replication of their original experiments. The benchmark-lottery perspective emphasizes that relative performance depends on benchmark choices \\citep{Lottery}. We instead hold task membership fixed in the principal comparison and vary the aggregation rule and allowed target response.\n\n\\paragraph{Aggregation and statistical robustness.}\n\\citet{SocialChoice} interpret tasks as voters and evaluation as social aggregation. Our comparison uses cardinal score summaries rather than implementing their ordinal voting framework. Classical robust estimation studies sensitivity to contamination of observations \\citep{Huber}. Resistance of a location estimate to contamination is not the same as support needed to exceed a rival's aggregation threshold under bounded upward changes. In particular, the target's order statistics and the rival threshold both matter here. We make no claim that the familiar mean, median, trimmed mean, exhaustive enumeration, or greedy policies are new.\n\n\\paragraph{Validity beyond a published score.}\nBenchmark leakage and rephrased contamination motivate taking strategic score changes seriously \\citep{Cheater,Rephrased}, but do not establish our assumed response law. Behavioral testing \\citep{CheckList}, truthfulness evaluation \\citep{TruthfulQA}, replication on fresh test sets \\citep{ImageNetReplication}, and underspecification \\citep{Underspecification} each illustrate a distinct reason not to equate one aggregate with general capability. Dataset and model documentation support stating intended uses and limitations \\citep{Datasheets,ModelCards}. We follow that principle by distinguishing a hypothetical score intervention from observed training.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparisons and uncertainty.}\nComparisons across datasets require attention to the experimental unit \\citep{Demsar}, and selection itself can bias performance evaluation \\citep{Cawley}. Accordingly, our cross-rule reversal is descriptive and conditional on the observed snapshots and a post-selected framing. Recorded bootstrap intervals \\citep{Efron} concern paired selection policies, not the reversal hypothesis. The reproduction environment specifies NumPy and SciPy \\citep{NumPy,SciPy}; the summaries use NumPy arrays.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/requirements.txt]]\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and response model}\nLet $X\\in[0,1]^{n\\times m}$ be a public score matrix, with one row per evaluated model and one coordinate per task. Fix a target row $t$, its score vector $x=X_t$, and all other rows as rivals. For support $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ and headroom fraction $\\alpha$, define\n\\begin{equation}\n x^{S,\\alpha}_j=x_j+\\alpha(1-x_j)\\,\\mathbf{1}\\{j\\in S\\}.\n \\label{eq:response}\n\\end{equation}\nAll selected coordinates receive the same fraction of remaining headroom. Unselected scores and all rival scores are unchanged. The evaluated ceilings are $\\alpha\\in\\{0.25,0.5,1\\}$. Full headroom allows a selected score to reach one; it is an optimistic stress condition, not an empirical prediction. Let $A_r$ denote aggregation rule $r$ and\n\\begin{equation}\n b_r=\\max_{i\\ne t} A_r(X_i),\\qquad\n \\cost_r(x,\\alpha)=\\min_S\\{|S|:A_r(x^{S,\\alpha})>b_r+\\eps\\},\n \\label{eq:cost}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\eps=10^{-10}$ implements strict first place. If no support wins, $\\cost_r=\\infty$. The normalized investment is $\\cost_r/m$ when attainable. A tie does not count as first place.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L16-L28]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nThis is an equal-coordinate cost model: intervening on a broad evaluation task and on a narrow subject each costs one within its own panel. Consequently, normalized support compares concentration within panels, not absolute work across datasets. It does not measure examples, money, accelerator time, or achievable learning gains. The assumptions exclude spillover, negative transfer, rivalry responses, and heterogeneity in the amount of work required to close headroom. Total score lift is available as a secondary diagnostic, but cannot calibrate those omitted quantities.\n\n\\subsection{Aggregation conditions and design rationale}\nFor sorted target values $z_{(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le z_{(m)}$, the mean is $m^{-1}\\sum_j z_j$. The conventional median takes the central order statistic for odd $m$ and averages the central pair for even $m$. The trimmed mean removes $q=\\lfloor m/5\\rfloor$ values from each end and averages the remaining $m-2q$. Tasks otherwise have equal weight. In the six-task panel this removes one value at each end, whereas on the subject panel trimming removes eleven at each end.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L70-L79]]\n\nThe research question is whether the relative minimum support for two rules changes with $\\alpha$. The mechanistic rationale is rank dependence: the mean rewards every positive uplift immediately; median and trimming rewards depend on where altered coordinates fall after sorting. Changing $\\alpha$ changes which ranks a coordinate can reach. It also changes each rule's attainable set. There is therefore no reason to interpret a difference computed only on currently successful targets as a pure cost difference without controlling the cohort. We assess response-dependent ordering directly, without attributing it to a new selection component.\n\nWithin any fixed rule and target, increasing $\\alpha$ cannot worsen attainability or increase the true minimum support: every coordinate and all three aggregators are monotone. This property does not constrain the sign of the difference between two rules' costs. A reversal of that difference is compatible with falling costs under both rules. Our primary contrast is the average median-minus-mean normalized support on targets attainable under both rules at both compared ceilings.\n\n\\subsection{Exact solutions, feasible witnesses, and bounds}\nThe frozen procedure begins by testing the full support. Since every allowed change is nonnegative and aggregation is coordinatewise monotone, failure of the full support certifies unattainability. This certificate is distinct from failure of a limited search. For a panel of at most six coordinates, supports are enumerated by cardinality, with deterministic semantic task-ID ordering. The first winning cardinality is exact. Thus every attainable primary six-task result used for the central contrast is a minimum-support result, not merely a successful heuristic plan.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L99-L117]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\nFor the mean on larger panels, sort headrooms $h_j=1-x_j$ from largest to smallest. Writing $h_{[j]}$ for this ordering, the minimum is the smallest $k$ satisfying\n\\begin{equation}\n \\frac{1}{m}\\sum_j x_j+\\frac{\\alpha}{m}\\sum_{j=1}^k h_{[j]}>b_{\\mathrm{mean}}+\\eps.\n \\label{eq:mean}\n\\end{equation}\nNo size-$k$ set can have greater summed headroom than this prefix, so a shorter failing prefix excludes all shorter supports. This explains exactness without invoking an unobserved solver behavior. Ties are resolved by semantic task identity.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L118-L125]]\n\nFor larger median and trimmed panels, the procedure greedily adds the coordinate maximizing the immediate updated aggregate, again breaking ties by semantic task identity. It stops at a verified strict-first support. Its size is an upper bound $U$, not necessarily $\\cost_r$. Independently computed conservative lower bounds $L$ yield $L\\le\\cost_r\\le U$. The reported $L$ is the evaluator's verified reference bound, not a claim that the greedy procedure computes an exact optimum. Mean costs and exhaustive small-panel costs collapse to points. Unattainable cases have no finite support interval; the machine-readable out-of-range sentinel is never plotted as an investment cost.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L126-L137]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L761-L789]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n\n\\subsection{Public score panels and experimental protocol}\nThe primary panels are HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 with 57 subject coordinates and the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates: IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro. The snapshots were acquired on 2026-11-17. The extracted records preserve original rows and settings; completeness and finite scores in the unit interval are required. These scores are not standardized effect sizes or observations from a homogeneous new evaluation. Source versions, checksums, filtering, and normalization are recorded in the supplementary reproduction specification.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/records.json]]\n\nModel aliases, normalized raw components, and score-map identities are grouped before partitioning the source records. The final evaluation uses a frozen, content-separated partition rather than a newly trained model. Within each family, rows tied for the lead under any studied rule are excluded from target selection. Remaining rows are sorted by mean score and divided into 24 bands; one target per band is sampled using each seed in $\\{1009,1013,1019\\}$. All rivals in the corresponding frozen pool remain visible. Each target is evaluated under every rule and ceiling, providing paired decisions rather than independently sampled rule-specific targets. Seeds vary target sampling, not model training.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L614-L687]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#seeds]]\n\nThe evaluator checks returned supports against the supplied matrix and accepts exactness only when independently supported. The ordinary primary cases define the reported target attainment rate: a verified strict-first support with a supported claim, divided by all primary targets in that cell. Constant-ceiling, zero-response, joint column-permutation, and reset-replay checks test specific consistency properties. Replay carries no statistical weight. The completed frozen evaluation satisfies its recorded qualification requirements; this does not certify generalization outside the snapshots or validate unverified auxiliary diagnostics.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json#protocol_policy]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n\n\\subsection{Comparators, conditioning, and uncertainty}\nMatched selection comparators are no intervention, a seeded random coordinate order, and the marginal-aggregate greedy policy. These are policies on the same target, panel, rule, and ceiling, not component ablations. For policy comparisons, define penalized effort $E=|S|/m$ for a verified successful support and $E=1$ otherwise. Assigning one to nonattainment is a reporting convention, not an observed minimum cost. Support plots instead condition on joint attainability and show genuine finite bounds.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L790-L818]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n\nThe central comparison keeps a fixed cohort per seed across both rules and both ceilings. The three-rule support plots use the within-ceiling joint cohort, which may differ across ceilings; seed means receive equal weight. We show individual seed summaries rather than inventing a population confidence interval. Existing policy intervals use 200 paired cluster resamples with resampling seed 48271 and percentile endpoints at 95\\%. They condition on the fixed snapshots and rival pools. They do not test differences between aggregation rules. Overlapping targets or related models across seeds further preclude treating these as independent training replications.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1046-L1068]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#paired_intervals]]\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Exact investment ordering changes with the response ceiling}\nOn the primary six-task panel, the fixed cohorts jointly attainable under mean and median at half and full headroom contain 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019, respectively. Figure~\\ref{fig:exact} shows the paired difference\n\\begin{equation}\n D_s(\\alpha)=\\frac{1}{|C_s|}\\sum_{t\\in C_s}\n \\frac{\\cost_{\\mathrm{median}}(X_t,\\alpha)-\\cost_{\\mathrm{mean}}(X_t,\\alpha)}{6}.\n\\end{equation}\nThe cohort $C_s$ is unchanged between panels. All included supports have exact minimum status backed by exhaustive selection. Therefore the comparison is about minimum investment in the specified model, not differences in heuristic success.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/exact_rule_contrast.pdf}\n\\caption{Exact paired contrast on fixed Open LLM cohorts. Negative values mean the median requires fewer modified coordinates on average. The same targets are retained at half and full headroom within each seed; cohort sizes are 18, 17, and 17. Points are descriptive seed summaries, not confidence intervals.}\n\\label{fig:exact}\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nAt half headroom, $D_s$ is $-0.1296$, $-0.1275$, and $-0.0686$ in seed order. At full headroom it is $+0.1389$, $+0.1275$, and $+0.1569$. Thus the average ordering reverses in every sampled cohort: median support is lower at half headroom and higher at full headroom. This does not mean each target reverses, nor does it estimate how frequently reversal would occur in a new leaderboard. The contrast was selected descriptively during analysis of the recorded results and is not a preregistered hypothesis test.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\nHolding the cohort fixed is important. At half headroom, some targets cannot win under one or both rules. Comparing the mean on all mean-attainable targets with the median on all median-attainable targets would conflate ranking resistance with selection of different models. Our fixed-cohort comparison removes that particular change in composition, while remaining conditional on joint attainability. Its claim is consequently narrower, but also cleaner: response ceilings can change rule ordering even without changing the compared targets.\n\n\\subsection{Attainability and support answer different questions}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:scope} includes all ordinary primary targets in its attainment panels. Each rule--ceiling cell contains 72 target decisions across the sampling seeds. On Open LLM, quarter headroom yields attainment for 11 targets under the mean, one under the median, and eight under trimming. At half headroom these counts are 65, 52, and 67; full headroom attains all 72 under every rule. A median can therefore have lower average cost on the jointly attainable cohort while excluding more targets overall. Neither quantity should be substituted for the other.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#grid]]\n\nOn HELM, quarter-headroom counts are nine, nine, and eight for mean, median, and trimming; half-headroom counts are 42, 42, and 41. All rules attain all 72 targets at full headroom. The low-ceiling nonattainment here is certified by the all-coordinate test. It is not a count of unresolved heuristic failures. These snapshots support a strong role for response magnitude, but do not identify the response magnitudes that actual post-training would achieve.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#grid]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/attainability_and_support_scope.pdf}\n\\caption{Attainability and certificate scope. Left: all ordinary primary targets, with one strict-first definition throughout. Right: equal-seed average support bounds on targets attainable under all three rules at that ceiling. HELM cohort sizes are $(14,13,13)$ at half and $(24,24,24)$ at full headroom; Open LLM sizes are $(18,17,17)$ and $(24,24,24)$. Bars are certification ranges, not statistical intervals; points indicate exact costs. Unlike Figure~\\ref{fig:exact}, right-panel cohorts can change across ceilings.}\n\\label{fig:scope}\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#grid]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#support]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe right panels expose the difference between exact and bounded evidence. On the HELM joint cohort at half headroom, the exact mean support fraction is $0.483$, while median and trimmed support intervals are $[0.018,0.364]$ and $[0.205,0.414]$. These averaged intervals lie below the exact mean cost on that cohort. This establishes a lower average cost than the mean there even without resolving the individual order-statistic optima. At full headroom, the mean cost is $0.279$, while median and trimming intervals are $[0.018,0.330]$ and $[0.153,0.304]$. These overlap the mean point, so a reversal of the large-panel minimum-cost ordering is not established. A feasible upper bound above the mean does not prove a higher minimum.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#support]]\n\nOn the six-task joint cohorts the intervals collapse. Half-headroom mean, median, and trimming costs are $0.676$, $0.568$, and $0.568$; the full-headroom values are $0.382$, $0.484$, and $0.421$. These within-ceiling summaries give context for the matched result, but they use different cohorts at the two ceilings. The primary reversal claim rests on Figure~\\ref{fig:exact}, not on comparing these marginal averages.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#support]]\n\n\\subsection{Selection-policy comparisons do not imply optimizer novelty}\nTable~\\ref{tab:policies} compares matched policies at half headroom over all targets, using penalized effort. The frozen procedure ties marginal greedy for every HELM rule and for the six-task mean. This is expected from their shared selection behavior in these conditions. On the six-task median and trimmed mean, exhaustive selection attains smaller penalized effort than the recorded greedy policy. The distinction matters: marginal gain can fail to anticipate the benefit of a set of coordinates jointly changing an order statistic.\n% @evidence[[derived/comparators.tex]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L109-L137]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Matched policy comparison at half headroom. Lower penalized effort is better; nonattainment contributes one. Exact/witness denotes exhaustive selection on the six-task panel, sorted headroom for means, and a feasible greedy witness for larger order-statistic panels. Values average all targets with equal seed weights.}\n\\label{tab:policies}\n\\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}\n\\toprule\nPanel & Rule & Exact/witness & Greedy & Random & None\\\\\n\\midrule\n% BEGIN GENERATED comparators\nHELM & Mean & 0.707 & 0.707 & 0.835 & 1.000 \\\\\nHELM & Median & 0.639 & 0.639 & 0.780 & 1.000 \\\\\nHELM & Trimmed & 0.670 & 0.670 & 0.819 & 1.000 \\\\\nOpen LLM & Mean & 0.764 & 0.764 & 0.822 & 1.000 \\\\\nOpen LLM & Median & 0.688 & 0.799 & 0.831 & 1.000 \\\\\nOpen LLM & Trimmed & 0.650 & 0.688 & 0.762 & 1.000 \\\\\n% END GENERATED comparators\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[derived/comparators.tex]]\n\\end{table}\n\nFor example, the six-task median has penalized effort $0.688$ versus greedy $0.799$ and random $0.831$; trimming has $0.650$, $0.688$, and $0.762$. No intervention yields one throughout, because target selection excludes existing leaders. These are observed policy comparisons under the response model, not improvements over a distinct evaluated architecture or a new exact algorithm. They also do not license claiming an average attainable minimum of $0.688$: that statistic includes unsuccessful targets by construction.\n% @evidence[[derived/comparators.tex]]\n\nRecorded cluster-resampling intervals for the policy contrasts, together with the complete rule--ceiling grid, appear in Supplementary Information. The intervals retain the unit of resampling used by the evaluator and are not reassigned to the cross-rule contrast. No controlled component ablation supports a mechanistic attribution to a proposed optimization refinement. The measured exact-versus-greedy difference is sufficient for the limited selection-policy conclusion.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#paired_intervals]]\n\n\\subsection{Scope checks and limits of the evaluated evidence}\nAuxiliary decisions include a six-coordinate HELM projection and a separately routed BBH panel with 24 recorded subtask coordinates. Open LLM auxiliary odd slots use BBH and even slots use the six-task family; those slots must not be interpreted as matched primary model identities. The BBH results are revision-matched but drawn from a distinct results snapshot, so matching model revisions does not establish identical evaluation settings. Their attainment and certificate counts are reported separately in the supplement, without pooling them into the primary findings.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L718-L732]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json#reconciliation]]\n% @evidence[[derived/auxiliary.tex]]\n\nThe final evaluation is completed and qualified, with recorded control pass fraction $1.0$. Qualification concerns the evaluator's supported claims and specified consistency checks, not optimality of every large-panel witness or unbiasedness of the post-selected scientific framing. Candidate-supplied diagnostics remain unverified; our measurements use recorded evaluator decisions and inspectable source instead. There is no failed final qualification to reinterpret as a performance result. The absence of a qualifying alternative in development does not invalidate the completed frozen comparison, and neither fact establishes general superiority.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_pass_rate]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/candidate_diagnostics_trust]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What investment means.}\nCounting coordinates isolates how aggregation rewards concentration, but it is not a training-production model. A unit of investment in a broad reasoning benchmark is not commensurate with a unit in an individual knowledge subject. Even within a panel, two tasks may have very different data access costs, learning curves, or contamination risks. Equal fractional headroom is useful because it is transparent, monotone, and bounded by the score range. It is not justified as a fitted account of any particular training process. Conclusions should therefore be stated conditionally: \\emph{if} these coordinate responses are available, this support suffices or is necessary.\n\n--- SUMMARY ---\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected Certified Attainability Frontiers for Targeted Leaderboard Investment; wrote matching idea and rubric bundles. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Only an untested proposal and the supplied incumbent aggregate are available, so no evidence-backed distilled recommendation is added.\n  - evidence: Supplied bootstrap correct=true and combined_score=0.53012102014295; no recent ideas or attempts. Named external incumbent source was unavailable; implementation details and failure causes remain unknown. Alternatives were perfect-score-only attacks, unsupported training-cost curves, and a wider aggregation-rule catalogue. Bounded score-response ceilings permit exact support-cost certificates using documented public score matrices. No experiments or literature searches were run. Only canonical local memory path ideas/01_certified_attainability_frontiers_for_targeted_leaderboard_investment/memory.md was created for this idea.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T12:50:05.435064+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.42832805393112416, correct=False; scientific acceptance failed; cause unavailable; correct=false alone does not establish a protocol or implementation defect; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.42832805393112416, \"control_pass_rate\": 0.9525462962962963, \"correct\": false, \"feasible_rate\": 0.4027777777777778, \"mean_effort_fraction\": 0.7963369070825209, \"mean_verified_gap_fraction\": 0.18981481481481483}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected Amortized Rank Window Certificates for Targeted Investment and wrote matching bundles. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=The proposal is untested, the recorded failure cause remains unavailable, and interface restrictions were already recorded rather than newly established scientific findings.\n  - evidence: Read named task/results/memory/skills and prior candidate source/spec. Interface supports uploaded matrices, mean/median/trimmed, common alpha, strict-first margin 1e-10, original-column outputs, work_seconds and node_budget. Prior source repeats whole-matrix aggregation per decision; amortization is a hypothesis, not a failure explanation. Prior combined_score=0.42832805393112416, correct=false, control_pass_rate=0.9525462962962963, feasible_rate=0.4027777777777778, mean_effort_fraction=0.7963369070825209, mean_verified_gap_fraction=0.18981481481481483; incumbent combined_score=0.53012102014295, correct=true. External incumbent source unavailable. Alternatives: repeated prefix-only certification, unsupported Borda/paper operations, and unobserved training costs. Selected conservative rank-window envelopes plus verified supports and shared preprocessing. No project code, tests, evaluator, literature search, or computational probes run. Canonical local target computed once after ID/title finalization: ideas/02_amortized_rank_window_certificates_for_targeted_investment/memory.md; no alternate same-cycle target created or used.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T13:06:05.197872+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.42913823911630933, correct=False; scientific acceptance failed; cause unavailable; correct=false alone does not establish a protocol or implementation defect; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.42913823911630933, \"control_pass_rate\": 0.9552469135802469, \"correct\": false, \"feasible_rate\": 0.4027777777777778, \"mean_effort_fraction\": 0.7963369070825209, \"mean_verified_gap_fraction\": 0.18981481481481483}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected Coupled Clipped Score Certificates for Targeted Investment and wrote matching bundles. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=The proposed envelope has not been implemented or evaluated, and available acceptance feedback does not establish a failure cause or a validated change in practical recommendations.\n  - evidence: Read named workflows/task/histories/manifest/memory/conclusions and prior candidate source/spec. Prior combined scores 0.42832805393112416 and 0.42913823911630933 both had correct=false; effort, feasibility, and verified-gap aggregates were unchanged, with slightly different control_pass_rate. Incumbent combined_score=0.53012102014295, correct=true. Failure cause unknown. Alternatives were additional caching, repeated prefix certification, costly exhaustive large-panel search, and unsupported Borda/training/paper operations. Selected a minimax hinge envelope coupling retained ranks through one support budget; its practical benefit is untested. No program/evaluator execution, computational probes, package installation, or literature search occurred. Canonical local directory finalized once after ID/title: ideas/03_coupled_clipped_score_certificates_for_targeted_investment/memory.md; no alternate same-cycle target or staging memory was created or used.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T13:18:02.192017+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.42832805393112416, correct=False; scientific acceptance failed; cause unavailable; correct=false alone does not establish a protocol or implementation defect; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.42832805393112416, \"control_pass_rate\": 0.9525462962962963, \"correct\": false, \"feasible_rate\": 0.4027777777777778, \"mean_effort_fraction\": 0.7963369070825209, \"mean_verified_gap_fraction\": 0.18981481481481483}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
