{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- INSTRUCTIONS ---\\\\n'; for parent in /AGENTS.md /workspace/AGENTS.md /workspace/paper/AGENTS.md; do if test -f \\\"\"'$parent\"; then printf '\"'\\\\n%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$parent\"; cat \"$parent\"; fi; done; printf '\"'\\\\n--- SCHEMA ---\\\\n'; cat reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- DRAFT RELEVANT PASSAGES ---\\\\n'; sed -n '35,125p' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- BIBLIOGRAPHY ---\\\\n'; cat references.bib; printf '\\\\n--- PROVENANCE STRUCTURE ---\\\\n'; jq 'type, (if type==\\\"object\\\" then keys else length end)' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY SKILL REMAINDER ---\\\\n'; sed -n '75,155p' .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; printf '\\\\n--- EXISTING MEMORY FILES ---\\\\n'; ls -l memory.md global_memory/memory.md global_memory/memory.jsonl conclusions.md 2>/dev/null\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- INSTRUCTIONS ---\\\\n'; for parent in /AGENTS.md /workspace/AGENTS.md /workspace/paper/AGENTS.md; do if test -f \\\"\"'$parent\"; then printf '\"'\\\\n%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$parent\"; cat \"$parent\"; fi; done; printf '\"'\\\\n--- SCHEMA ---\\\\n'; cat reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- DRAFT RELEVANT PASSAGES ---\\\\n'; sed -n '35,125p' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- BIBLIOGRAPHY ---\\\\n'; cat references.bib; printf '\\\\n--- PROVENANCE STRUCTURE ---\\\\n'; jq 'type, (if type==\\\"object\\\" then keys else length end)' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY SKILL REMAINDER ---\\\\n'; sed -n '75,155p' .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; printf '\\\\n--- EXISTING MEMORY FILES ---\\\\n'; ls -l memory.md global_memory/memory.md global_memory/memory.jsonl conclusions.md 2>/dev/null\"","aggregated_output":"\n--- INSTRUCTIONS ---\n\n--- SCHEMA ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Conditional task-support investment needed for a fixed target model to become strictly first on public multi-task score matrices under mean, median, and trimmed-mean aggregation, with bounded score uplifts and explicit attainment certificates.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Fixed-rival score-space intervention: selected coordinates change from x_j to x_j + alpha*(1-x_j), with alpha in {0.25, 0.5, 1}; investment is selected-task cardinality or its panel-normalized fraction, not measured training cost\",\n    \"Equal-weight arithmetic mean, conventional median averaging the two middle values for even panels, and symmetric trimmed mean dropping floor(0.2*m) scores from each end\",\n    \"Exact sorted-headroom support selection for the mean and exhaustive support enumeration for panels of at most six tasks\",\n    \"Deterministic marginal-aggregate greedy support selection for larger median and trimmed-mean panels, verified against fixed rivals with strict-first tolerance 1e-10\",\n    \"Conservative lower bounds and feasible support witnesses distinguishing optimal, bounded/unresolved, and infeasible decisions\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Robust statistics and finite-sample sensitivity of medians and trimmed means; estimator robustness is not automatically leaderboard manipulation resistance\",\n    \"Social-choice approaches to multi-task agent evaluation; ordinal aggregation is related work, not an implemented comparator\",\n    \"Strategic post-training incentives; contrast multi-developer strategic models with this run's single-target, fixed-rival counterfactual\",\n    \"Task-selection sensitivity and the benchmark lottery\",\n    \"Evaluation-data contamination as motivation and a limitation of interpreting assumed score uplifts causally\",\n    \"Executed no-intervention, random-order, and marginal-greedy support baselines; no separate generic optimization literature search is needed for these elementary controls\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"HELM MMLU release v1.13.0: 57 subject-score coordinates\",\n    \"Open LLM Leaderboard contents snapshot 9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2: six raw-score coordinates, IFEval, BBH, MATH Lvl 5, GPQA, MUSR, MMLU-PRO\",\n    \"Open LLM Leaderboard results snapshot aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5: separately reported complete 24-subtask BBH panels with exact model-revision matches; not pooled with primary six-task contents scores\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"Conditional attainability under response ceilings: final evaluation reports both attainment shortfalls and attained targets; alpha is an assumed score-response parameter, not a measured training response\",\n    \"Exact small-panel certification versus nonzero aggregate certificate gaps on the evaluated workload; a feasible greedy witness need not establish minimum support\",\n    \"Task granularity and panel selection delimit interpretation across subject-level MMLU, six-task contents scores, and separate BBH sensitivity panels; this is not architecture transfer\",\n    \"No qualifying search winner was found and the baseline was selected; proposed rank-window and coupled-clipped variants do not support claims of methodological improvement\",\n    \"No evidence establishes a universal robustness ordering among the three aggregation rules or an empirical cost of actual contamination\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"median trimmed mean finite-sample replacement breakdown order statistics\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Find foundational robust-statistics treatment of the actual order-statistic aggregators and sensitivity to changing a subset of coordinates. Use only independently screened foundational sources, not leaderboard-specific discovery. Breakdown is a conceptual comparator, not the same estimand as bounded one-sided support cost.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory\\\" 2312.03121\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference positions aggregation of multi-task evaluations within social choice. Contrast ordinal/rank aggregation with the run's cardinal mean, median, and trimmed mean without implying that Borda was tested. Resolve only the canonical supplied record.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Leaderboard Incentives: Model Rankings under Strategic Post-Training\\\" 2603.08371\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference supplies the nearest named incentive-model comparator. Check its actual assumptions later, distinguishing strategic interaction among developers from this run's fixed rivals and exogenous score-uplift ceilings. No equilibrium or training effect is inferred here.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"The Benchmark Lottery\\\" 2107.07002\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference contextualizes task-panel sensitivity. The workload contains full and six-coordinate panels plus a separate BBH sensitivity panel; task selection and aggregation should not be conflated, and no rule-order reversal is claimed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding\\\" 2009.03300\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Ground the 57-subject MMLU task structure used by the HELM score matrix. The benchmark paper does not by itself establish the provenance or settings of the HELM v1.13.0 snapshot.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Holistic Evaluation of Language Models\\\" 2211.09110\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Ground the evaluation framework supplying the HELM public results. Pair the canonical paper with already-recorded v1.13.0 source metadata; do not assume identical evaluation settings across all model rows.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them\\\" 2210.09261\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Identify BBH as the source of the separately reported 24-subtask score panel. The paper title is benchmark context only: this run does not compare chain-of-thought prompting or architectures, and it does not justify treating contents and results runs as interchangeable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"open-llm-leaderboard/contents\\\" \\\"9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2\\\"\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Resolve citation metadata directly from the official pinned dataset record for the six raw-score coordinates. Use the supplied canonical Hugging Face dataset revision rather than broad search if needed; retain the separate results revision aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5 for BBH provenance. Do not invent an associated paper or data license.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater\\\" 2311.01964\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference motivates evaluation-data exposure while marking the key causal gap: the run manipulates published score coordinates and neither trains models nor observes contamination-induced improvements. Its findings must be read before any substantive citation is written.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any attempt to identify or discover the prohibited prior study by title, authors, results, or paraphrases of the research question, including broad searches combining targeted investment, leaderboard manipulation, and aggregation rules\",\n    \"Citation-graph expansion, cited-by searches, related-paper recommendations, surveys, discussion pages, mirrors, code repositories, or social posts that could expose the prohibited study; do not inspect reference lists to discover neighbors\",\n    \"Broad strategic leaderboard or benchmark-cheating discovery searches; use only exact supplied starter identifiers and primary dataset records for those topics\",\n    \"Searches for Certified Attainability Frontiers, Amortized Rank Window Certificates, or Coupled Clipped Score Certificates as if these run-local proposal labels were established literature or successful methods\",\n    \"Queries claiming tested Borda, game-theoretic equilibria, adversarial training, training-cost curves, observed contamination effects, or new optimization bounds absent from the selected-method evidence\",\n    \"Architecture, representation-learning, or chain-of-thought transition queries; no controlled architecture or prompting transition was evaluated\",\n    \"Queries presupposing universal median robustness, an aggregation-order reversal, cross-benchmark transfer, or superiority of an unqualified candidate\",\n    \"Generic optimization, generic benchmarking, scientific method, paper-writing, citation-volume, or campaign-chronology queries\",\n    \"Unpinned latest leaderboard searches, Arena-specific detours, or standalone searches for every component benchmark without a corresponding scientific comparison\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n\n--- DRAFT RELEVANT PASSAGES ---\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Evaluation breadth and benchmark composition.}\nMMLU evaluates knowledge across subjects \\citep{MMLU}; HELM emphasizes broad, transparent evaluation across scenarios and metrics \\citep{HELM}. BIG-bench and BBH characterize diverse tasks and difficult reasoning settings \\citep{BIGBench,BBH}. The six-task panel studied here combines instruction following \\citep{IFEval}, challenging mathematics \\citep{MATH}, expert question answering \\citep{GPQA}, narrative reasoning \\citep{MuSR}, and knowledge-oriented reasoning \\citep{MMLUPro}, alongside BBH. These source works explain the tasks' purposes; our empirical claims concern the pinned score snapshots, not a replication of their original experiments. The benchmark-lottery perspective emphasizes that relative performance depends on benchmark choices \\citep{Lottery}. We instead hold task membership fixed in the principal comparison and vary the aggregation rule and allowed target response.\n\n\\paragraph{Aggregation and statistical robustness.}\n\\citet{SocialChoice} interpret tasks as voters and evaluation as social aggregation. Our comparison uses cardinal score summaries rather than implementing their ordinal voting framework. Classical robust estimation studies sensitivity to contamination of observations \\citep{Huber}. Resistance of a location estimate to contamination is not the same as support needed to exceed a rival's aggregation threshold under bounded upward changes. In particular, the target's order statistics and the rival threshold both matter here. We make no claim that the familiar mean, median, trimmed mean, exhaustive enumeration, or greedy policies are new.\n\n\\paragraph{Validity beyond a published score.}\nBenchmark leakage and rephrased contamination motivate taking strategic score changes seriously \\citep{Cheater,Rephrased}, but do not establish our assumed response law. Behavioral testing \\citep{CheckList}, truthfulness evaluation \\citep{TruthfulQA}, replication on fresh test sets \\citep{ImageNetReplication}, and underspecification \\citep{Underspecification} each illustrate a distinct reason not to equate one aggregate with general capability. Dataset and model documentation support stating intended uses and limitations \\citep{Datasheets,ModelCards}. We follow that principle by distinguishing a hypothetical score intervention from observed training.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparisons and uncertainty.}\nComparisons across datasets require attention to the experimental unit \\citep{Demsar}, and selection itself can bias performance evaluation \\citep{Cawley}. Accordingly, our cross-rule reversal is descriptive and conditional on the observed snapshots and a post-selected framing. Recorded bootstrap intervals \\citep{Efron} concern paired selection policies, not the reversal hypothesis. The reproduction environment specifies NumPy and SciPy \\citep{NumPy,SciPy}; the summaries use NumPy arrays.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/requirements.txt]]\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and response model}\nLet $X\\in[0,1]^{n\\times m}$ be a public score matrix, with one row per evaluated model and one coordinate per task. Fix a target row $t$, its score vector $x=X_t$, and all other rows as rivals. For support $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ and headroom fraction $\\alpha$, define\n\\begin{equation}\n x^{S,\\alpha}_j=x_j+\\alpha(1-x_j)\\,\\mathbf{1}\\{j\\in S\\}.\n \\label{eq:response}\n\\end{equation}\nAll selected coordinates receive the same fraction of remaining headroom. Unselected scores and all rival scores are unchanged. The evaluated ceilings are $\\alpha\\in\\{0.25,0.5,1\\}$. Full headroom allows a selected score to reach one; it is an optimistic stress condition, not an empirical prediction. Let $A_r$ denote aggregation rule $r$ and\n\\begin{equation}\n b_r=\\max_{i\\ne t} A_r(X_i),\\qquad\n \\cost_r(x,\\alpha)=\\min_S\\{|S|:A_r(x^{S,\\alpha})>b_r+\\eps\\},\n \\label{eq:cost}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\eps=10^{-10}$ implements strict first place. If no support wins, $\\cost_r=\\infty$. The normalized investment is $\\cost_r/m$ when attainable. A tie does not count as first place.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L16-L28]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nThis is an equal-coordinate cost model: intervening on a broad evaluation task and on a narrow subject each costs one within its own panel. Consequently, normalized support compares concentration within panels, not absolute work across datasets. It does not measure examples, money, accelerator time, or achievable learning gains. The assumptions exclude spillover, negative transfer, rivalry responses, and heterogeneity in the amount of work required to close headroom. Total score lift is available as a secondary diagnostic, but cannot calibrate those omitted quantities.\n\n\\subsection{Aggregation conditions and design rationale}\nFor sorted target values $z_{(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le z_{(m)}$, the mean is $m^{-1}\\sum_j z_j$. The conventional median takes the central order statistic for odd $m$ and averages the central pair for even $m$. The trimmed mean removes $q=\\lfloor m/5\\rfloor$ values from each end and averages the remaining $m-2q$. Tasks otherwise have equal weight. In the six-task panel this removes one value at each end, whereas on the subject panel trimming removes eleven at each end.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L70-L79]]\n\nThe research question is whether the relative minimum support for two rules changes with $\\alpha$. The mechanistic rationale is rank dependence: the mean rewards every positive uplift immediately; median and trimming rewards depend on where altered coordinates fall after sorting. Changing $\\alpha$ changes which ranks a coordinate can reach. It also changes each rule's attainable set. There is therefore no reason to interpret a difference computed only on currently successful targets as a pure cost difference without controlling the cohort. We assess response-dependent ordering directly, without attributing it to a new selection component.\n\nWithin any fixed rule and target, increasing $\\alpha$ cannot worsen attainability or increase the true minimum support: every coordinate and all three aggregators are monotone. This property does not constrain the sign of the difference between two rules' costs. A reversal of that difference is compatible with falling costs under both rules. Our primary contrast is the average median-minus-mean normalized support on targets attainable under both rules at both compared ceilings.\n\n\\subsection{Exact solutions, feasible witnesses, and bounds}\nThe frozen procedure begins by testing the full support. Since every allowed change is nonnegative and aggregation is coordinatewise monotone, failure of the full support certifies unattainability. This certificate is distinct from failure of a limited search. For a panel of at most six coordinates, supports are enumerated by cardinality, with deterministic semantic task-ID ordering. The first winning cardinality is exact. Thus every attainable primary six-task result used for the central contrast is a minimum-support result, not merely a successful heuristic plan.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L99-L117]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\nFor the mean on larger panels, sort headrooms $h_j=1-x_j$ from largest to smallest. Writing $h_{[j]}$ for this ordering, the minimum is the smallest $k$ satisfying\n\\begin{equation}\n \\frac{1}{m}\\sum_j x_j+\\frac{\\alpha}{m}\\sum_{j=1}^k h_{[j]}>b_{\\mathrm{mean}}+\\eps.\n \\label{eq:mean}\n\\end{equation}\nNo size-$k$ set can have greater summed headroom than this prefix, so a shorter failing prefix excludes all shorter supports. This explains exactness without invoking an unobserved solver behavior. Ties are resolved by semantic task identity.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L118-L125]]\n\nFor larger median and trimmed panels, the procedure greedily adds the coordinate maximizing the immediate updated aggregate, again breaking ties by semantic task identity. It stops at a verified strict-first support. Its size is an upper bound $U$, not necessarily $\\cost_r$. Independently computed conservative lower bounds $L$ yield $L\\le\\cost_r\\le U$. The reported $L$ is the evaluator's verified reference bound, not a claim that the greedy procedure computes an exact optimum. Mean costs and exhaustive small-panel costs collapse to points. Unattainable cases have no finite support interval; the machine-readable out-of-range sentinel is never plotted as an investment cost.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L126-L137]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L761-L789]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n\n\\subsection{Public score panels and experimental protocol}\nThe primary panels are HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 with 57 subject coordinates and the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates: IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro. The snapshots were acquired on 2026-11-17. The extracted records preserve original rows and settings; completeness and finite scores in the unit interval are required. These scores are not standardized effect sizes or observations from a homogeneous new evaluation. Source versions, checksums, filtering, and normalization are recorded in the supplementary reproduction specification.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/records.json]]\n\nModel aliases, normalized raw components, and score-map identities are grouped before partitioning the source records. The final evaluation uses a frozen, content-separated partition rather than a newly trained model. Within each family, rows tied for the lead under any studied rule are excluded from target selection. Remaining rows are sorted by mean score and divided into 24 bands; one target per band is sampled using each seed in $\\{1009,1013,1019\\}$. All rivals in the corresponding frozen pool remain visible. Each target is evaluated under every rule and ceiling, providing paired decisions rather than independently sampled rule-specific targets. Seeds vary target sampling, not model training.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L614-L687]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#seeds]]\n\nThe evaluator checks returned supports against the supplied matrix and accepts exactness only when independently supported. The ordinary primary cases define the reported target attainment rate: a verified strict-first support with a supported claim, divided by all primary targets in that cell. Constant-ceiling, zero-response, joint column-permutation, and reset-replay checks test specific consistency properties. Replay carries no statistical weight. The completed frozen evaluation satisfies its recorded qualification requirements; this does not certify generalization outside the snapshots or validate unverified auxiliary diagnostics.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json#protocol_policy]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n\n\\subsection{Comparators, conditioning, and uncertainty}\nMatched selection comparators are no intervention, a seeded random coordinate order, and the marginal-aggregate greedy policy. These are policies on the same target, panel, rule, and ceiling, not component ablations. For policy comparisons, define penalized effort $E=|S|/m$ for a verified successful support and $E=1$ otherwise. Assigning one to nonattainment is a reporting convention, not an observed minimum cost. Support plots instead condition on joint attainability and show genuine finite bounds.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L790-L818]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n\nThe central comparison keeps a fixed cohort per seed across both rules and both ceilings. The three-rule support plots use the within-ceiling joint cohort, which may differ across ceilings; seed means receive equal weight. We show individual seed summaries rather than inventing a population confidence interval. Existing policy intervals use 200 paired cluster resamples with resampling seed 48271 and percentile endpoints at 95\\%. They condition on the fixed snapshots and rival pools. They do not test differences between aggregation rules. Overlapping targets or related models across seeds further preclude treating these as independent training replications.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1046-L1068]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#paired_intervals]]\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Exact investment ordering changes with the response ceiling}\nOn the primary six-task panel, the fixed cohorts jointly attainable under mean and median at half and full headroom contain 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019, respectively. Figure~\\ref{fig:exact} shows the paired difference\n\\begin{equation}\n D_s(\\alpha)=\\frac{1}{|C_s|}\\sum_{t\\in C_s}\n \\frac{\\cost_{\\mathrm{median}}(X_t,\\alpha)-\\cost_{\\mathrm{mean}}(X_t,\\alpha)}{6}.\n\\end{equation}\n\n--- BIBLIOGRAPHY ---\n@article{MMLU,\n title={{Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding}},\n author={Hendrycks, Dan and Burns, Collin and Basart, Steven and Zou, Andy and Mazeika, Mantas and Song, Dawn and Steinhardt, Jacob},\n year={2020},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2009.03300},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.03300}\n}\n\n@article{BBH,\n title={{Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them}},\n author={Suzgun, Mirac and Scales, Nathan and Schärli, Nathanael and Gehrmann, Sebastian and Tay, Yi and Chung, Hyung Won and others},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2210.09261},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.09261}\n}\n\n@article{HELM,\n title={{Holistic Evaluation of Language Models}},\n author={Liang, Percy and Bommasani, Rishi and Lee, Tony and Tsipras, Dimitris and Soylu, Dilara and Yasunaga, Michihiro and others},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2211.09110},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09110}\n}\n\n@article{Lottery,\n title={{The Benchmark Lottery}},\n author={Dehghani, Mostafa and Tay, Yi and Gritsenko, Alexey A. and Zhao, Zhe and Houlsby, Neil and Diaz, Fernando and Metzler, Donald and Vinyals, Oriol},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2107.07002},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07002}\n}\n\n@article{SocialChoice,\n title={{Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory}},\n author={Lanctot, Marc and Larson, Kate and Bachrach, Yoram and Marris, Luke and Li, Zun and Bhoopchand, Avishkar and others},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2312.03121},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03121}\n}\n\n@article{Cheater,\n title={{Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater}},\n author={Zhou, Kun and Zhu, Yutao and Chen, Zhipeng and Chen, Wentong and Zhao, Wayne Xin and Chen, Xu and others},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.01964},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01964}\n}\n\n@article{Rephrased,\n title={{Rethinking Benchmark and Contamination for Language Models with Rephrased Samples}},\n author={Yang, Shuo and Chiang, Wei-Lin and Zheng, Lianmin and Gonzalez, Joseph E. and Stoica, Ion},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.04850},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.04850}\n}\n\n@article{GPQA,\n title={{GPQA: A Graduate-Level Google-Proof Q\\&A Benchmark}},\n author={Rein, David and Hou, Betty Li and Stickland, Asa Cooper and Petty, Jackson and Pang, Richard Yuanzhe and Dirani, Julien and Michael, Julian and Bowman, Samuel R.},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.12022},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.12022}\n}\n\n@article{IFEval,\n title={{Instruction-Following Evaluation for Large Language Models}},\n author={Zhou, Jeffrey and Lu, Tianjian and Mishra, Swaroop and Brahma, Siddhartha and Basu, Sujoy and Luan, Yi and Zhou, Denny and Hou, Le},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.07911},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.07911}\n}\n\n@article{MMLUPro,\n title={{MMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark}},\n author={Wang, Yubo and Ma, Xueguang and Zhang, Ge and Ni, Yuansheng and Chandra, Abhranil and Guo, Shiguang and others},\n year={2024},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.01574},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.01574}\n}\n\n@article{MATH,\n title={{Measuring Mathematical Problem Solving With the MATH Dataset}},\n author={Hendrycks, Dan and Burns, Collin and Kadavath, Saurav and Arora, Akul and Basart, Steven and Tang, Eric and Song, Dawn and Steinhardt, Jacob},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2103.03874},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.03874}\n}\n\n@article{MuSR,\n title={{MuSR: Testing the Limits of Chain-of-thought with Multistep Soft Reasoning}},\n author={Sprague, Zayne and Ye, Xi and Bostrom, Kaj and Chaudhuri, Swarat and Durrett, Greg},\n year={2023},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.16049},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.16049}\n}\n\n@article{BIGBench,\n title={{Beyond the Imitation Game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models}},\n author={Srivastava, Aarohi and Rastogi, Abhinav and Rao, Abhishek and Shoeb, Abu Awal Md and Abid, Abubakar and Fisch, Adam and others},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2206.04615},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.04615}\n}\n\n@article{Datasheets,\n title={{Datasheets for Datasets}},\n author={Gebru, Timnit and Morgenstern, Jamie and Vecchione, Briana and Vaughan, Jennifer Wortman and Wallach, Hanna and Daumé III, Hal and Crawford, Kate},\n year={2018},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1803.09010},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010}\n}\n\n@article{ModelCards,\n title={{Model Cards for Model Reporting}},\n author={Mitchell, Margaret and Wu, Simone and Zaldivar, Andrew and Barnes, Parker and Vasserman, Lucy and Hutchinson, Ben and others},\n year={2018},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1810.03993},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993}\n}\n\n@article{CheckList,\n title={{Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP models with CheckList}},\n author={Ribeiro, Marco Tulio and Wu, Tongshuang and Guestrin, Carlos and Singh, Sameer},\n year={2020},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2005.04118},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.04118}\n}\n\n@article{Underspecification,\n title={{Underspecification Presents Challenges for Credibility in Modern Machine Learning}},\n author={D'Amour, Alexander and Heller, Katherine and Moldovan, Dan and Adlam, Ben and Alipanahi, Babak and Beutel, Alex and others},\n year={2020},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2011.03395},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.03395}\n}\n\n@article{ImageNetReplication,\n title={{Do ImageNet Classifiers Generalize to ImageNet?}},\n author={Recht, Benjamin and Roelofs, Rebecca and Schmidt, Ludwig and Shankar, Vaishaal},\n year={2019},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1902.10811},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10811}\n}\n\n@article{TruthfulQA,\n title={{TruthfulQA: Measuring How Models Mimic Human Falsehoods}},\n author={Lin, Stephanie and Hilton, Jacob and Evans, Owain},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2109.07958},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07958}\n}\n\n@article{NumPy,\n title={{Array Programming with NumPy}},\n author={Harris, Charles R. and Millman, K. Jarrod and van der Walt, Stéfan J. and Gommers, Ralf and Virtanen, Pauli and Cournapeau, David and others},\n year={2020},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2006.10256},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.10256}\n}\n\n@article{SciPy,\n title={{SciPy 1.0--Fundamental Algorithms for Scientific Computing in Python}},\n author={Virtanen, Pauli and Gommers, Ralf and Oliphant, Travis E. and Haberland, Matt and Reddy, Tyler and Cournapeau, David and others},\n year={2019},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1907.10121},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.10121}\n}\n\n@article{Demsar,\n title={{Statistical Comparisons of Classifiers over Multiple Data Sets}},\n author={Janez DemÅ¡ar},\n year={2006},\n journal={Journal of Machine Learning Research},\n url={https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v7/demsar06a.html}\n}\n\n@article{Cawley,\n title={{On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation}},\n author={Gavin C. Cawley and Nicola L. C. Talbot},\n year={2010},\n journal={Journal of Machine Learning Research},\n url={https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html}\n}\n\n@article{Huber,\n title={{Robust Estimation of a Location Parameter}},\n author={Peter J. Huber},\n year={1964},\n journal={The Annals of Mathematical Statistics},\n doi={10.1214/aoms/1177703732}\n}\n\n@article{Efron,\n title={{Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife}},\n author={B. Efron},\n year={1979},\n journal={The Annals of Statistics},\n doi={10.1214/aos/1176344552}\n}\n\n--- PROVENANCE STRUCTURE ---\n\"object\"\n[\n  \"count\",\n  \"generated_at\",\n  \"queries\",\n  \"references\"\n]\n\n--- MEMORY SKILL REMAINDER ---\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n\n--- EXISTING MEMORY FILES ---\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 26179 Nov 17 14:14 global_memory/memory.jsonl\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 25816 Nov 17 14:14 global_memory/memory.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent 12787 Nov 17 14:14 memory.md\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
