{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- COMPLETE SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\\\\n'; cat reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- DRAFT CITATION TARGETS ---\\\\n'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '68,121p'; printf '\\\\n--- BIBLIOGRAPHY TITLES ---\\\\n'; rg '\"'^@| title='\"' references.bib; printf '\\\\n--- PROVENANCE RECORD SAMPLE ---\\\\n'; jq '.references[0], .queries' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- SUMMARY RESTRICTIONS AND SOURCES ---\\\\n'; rg -n -i 'prohibit|citation|reference|open.llm|snapshot|raw.score|no.*expansion' global_memory_summary.md paper_strategy.json; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY HELPER ---\\\\n'; sed -n '1,160p' .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- COMPLETE SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\\\\n'; cat reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- DRAFT CITATION TARGETS ---\\\\n'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '68,121p'; printf '\\\\n--- BIBLIOGRAPHY TITLES ---\\\\n'; rg '\"'^@| title='\"' references.bib; printf '\\\\n--- PROVENANCE RECORD SAMPLE ---\\\\n'; jq '.references[0], .queries' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- SUMMARY RESTRICTIONS AND SOURCES ---\\\\n'; rg -n -i 'prohibit|citation|reference|open.llm|snapshot|raw.score|no.*expansion' global_memory_summary.md paper_strategy.json; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY HELPER ---\\\\n'; sed -n '1,160p' .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py\"","aggregated_output":"\n--- COMPLETE SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Conditional task-support investment needed for a fixed target model to become strictly first on public multi-task score matrices under mean, median, and trimmed-mean aggregation, with bounded score uplifts and explicit attainment certificates.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Fixed-rival score-space intervention: selected coordinates change from x_j to x_j + alpha*(1-x_j), with alpha in {0.25, 0.5, 1}; investment is selected-task cardinality or its panel-normalized fraction, not measured training cost\",\n    \"Equal-weight arithmetic mean, conventional median averaging the two middle values for even panels, and symmetric trimmed mean dropping floor(0.2*m) scores from each end\",\n    \"Exact sorted-headroom support selection for the mean and exhaustive support enumeration for panels of at most six tasks\",\n    \"Deterministic marginal-aggregate greedy support selection for larger median and trimmed-mean panels, verified against fixed rivals with strict-first tolerance 1e-10\",\n    \"Conservative lower bounds and feasible support witnesses distinguishing optimal, bounded/unresolved, and infeasible decisions\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Robust statistics and finite-sample sensitivity of medians and trimmed means; estimator robustness is not automatically leaderboard manipulation resistance\",\n    \"Social-choice approaches to multi-task agent evaluation; ordinal aggregation is related work, not an implemented comparator\",\n    \"Strategic post-training incentives; contrast multi-developer strategic models with this run's single-target, fixed-rival counterfactual\",\n    \"Task-selection sensitivity and the benchmark lottery\",\n    \"Evaluation-data contamination as motivation and a limitation of interpreting assumed score uplifts causally\",\n    \"Executed no-intervention, random-order, and marginal-greedy support baselines; no separate generic optimization literature search is needed for these elementary controls\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"HELM MMLU release v1.13.0: 57 subject-score coordinates\",\n    \"Open LLM Leaderboard contents snapshot 9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2: six raw-score coordinates, IFEval, BBH, MATH Lvl 5, GPQA, MUSR, MMLU-PRO\",\n    \"Open LLM Leaderboard results snapshot aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5: separately reported complete 24-subtask BBH panels with exact model-revision matches; not pooled with primary six-task contents scores\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"Conditional attainability under response ceilings: final evaluation reports both attainment shortfalls and attained targets; alpha is an assumed score-response parameter, not a measured training response\",\n    \"Exact small-panel certification versus nonzero aggregate certificate gaps on the evaluated workload; a feasible greedy witness need not establish minimum support\",\n    \"Task granularity and panel selection delimit interpretation across subject-level MMLU, six-task contents scores, and separate BBH sensitivity panels; this is not architecture transfer\",\n    \"No qualifying search winner was found and the baseline was selected; proposed rank-window and coupled-clipped variants do not support claims of methodological improvement\",\n    \"No evidence establishes a universal robustness ordering among the three aggregation rules or an empirical cost of actual contamination\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"median trimmed mean finite-sample replacement breakdown order statistics\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Find foundational robust-statistics treatment of the actual order-statistic aggregators and sensitivity to changing a subset of coordinates. Use only independently screened foundational sources, not leaderboard-specific discovery. Breakdown is a conceptual comparator, not the same estimand as bounded one-sided support cost.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory\\\" 2312.03121\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference positions aggregation of multi-task evaluations within social choice. Contrast ordinal/rank aggregation with the run's cardinal mean, median, and trimmed mean without implying that Borda was tested. Resolve only the canonical supplied record.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Leaderboard Incentives: Model Rankings under Strategic Post-Training\\\" 2603.08371\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference supplies the nearest named incentive-model comparator. Check its actual assumptions later, distinguishing strategic interaction among developers from this run's fixed rivals and exogenous score-uplift ceilings. No equilibrium or training effect is inferred here.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"The Benchmark Lottery\\\" 2107.07002\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference contextualizes task-panel sensitivity. The workload contains full and six-coordinate panels plus a separate BBH sensitivity panel; task selection and aggregation should not be conflated, and no rule-order reversal is claimed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding\\\" 2009.03300\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Ground the 57-subject MMLU task structure used by the HELM score matrix. The benchmark paper does not by itself establish the provenance or settings of the HELM v1.13.0 snapshot.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Holistic Evaluation of Language Models\\\" 2211.09110\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Ground the evaluation framework supplying the HELM public results. Pair the canonical paper with already-recorded v1.13.0 source metadata; do not assume identical evaluation settings across all model rows.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them\\\" 2210.09261\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Identify BBH as the source of the separately reported 24-subtask score panel. The paper title is benchmark context only: this run does not compare chain-of-thought prompting or architectures, and it does not justify treating contents and results runs as interchangeable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"open-llm-leaderboard/contents\\\" \\\"9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2\\\"\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Resolve citation metadata directly from the official pinned dataset record for the six raw-score coordinates. Use the supplied canonical Hugging Face dataset revision rather than broad search if needed; retain the separate results revision aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5 for BBH provenance. Do not invent an associated paper or data license.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater\\\" 2311.01964\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An explicitly permitted starter reference motivates evaluation-data exposure while marking the key causal gap: the run manipulates published score coordinates and neither trains models nor observes contamination-induced improvements. Its findings must be read before any substantive citation is written.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any attempt to identify or discover the prohibited prior study by title, authors, results, or paraphrases of the research question, including broad searches combining targeted investment, leaderboard manipulation, and aggregation rules\",\n    \"Citation-graph expansion, cited-by searches, related-paper recommendations, surveys, discussion pages, mirrors, code repositories, or social posts that could expose the prohibited study; do not inspect reference lists to discover neighbors\",\n    \"Broad strategic leaderboard or benchmark-cheating discovery searches; use only exact supplied starter identifiers and primary dataset records for those topics\",\n    \"Searches for Certified Attainability Frontiers, Amortized Rank Window Certificates, or Coupled Clipped Score Certificates as if these run-local proposal labels were established literature or successful methods\",\n    \"Queries claiming tested Borda, game-theoretic equilibria, adversarial training, training-cost curves, observed contamination effects, or new optimization bounds absent from the selected-method evidence\",\n    \"Architecture, representation-learning, or chain-of-thought transition queries; no controlled architecture or prompting transition was evaluated\",\n    \"Queries presupposing universal median robustness, an aggregation-order reversal, cross-benchmark transfer, or superiority of an unqualified candidate\",\n    \"Generic optimization, generic benchmarking, scientific method, paper-writing, citation-volume, or campaign-chronology queries\",\n    \"Unpinned latest leaderboard searches, Arena-specific detours, or standalone searches for every component benchmark without a corresponding scientific comparison\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n\n--- DRAFT CITATION TARGETS ---\n    68\tThis is an equal-coordinate cost model: intervening on a broad evaluation task and on a narrow subject each costs one within its own panel. Consequently, normalized support compares concentration within panels, not absolute work across datasets. It does not measure examples, money, accelerator time, or achievable learning gains. The assumptions exclude spillover, negative transfer, rivalry responses, and heterogeneity in the amount of work required to close headroom. Total score lift is available as a secondary diagnostic, but cannot calibrate those omitted quantities.\n    69\t\n    70\t\\subsection{Aggregation conditions and design rationale}\n    71\tFor sorted target values $z_{(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le z_{(m)}$, the mean is $m^{-1}\\sum_j z_j$. The conventional median takes the central order statistic for odd $m$ and averages the central pair for even $m$. The trimmed mean removes $q=\\lfloor m/5\\rfloor$ values from each end and averages the remaining $m-2q$. Tasks otherwise have equal weight. In the six-task panel this removes one value at each end, whereas on the subject panel trimming removes eleven at each end.\n    72\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L70-L79]]\n    73\t\n    74\tThe research question is whether the relative minimum support for two rules changes with $\\alpha$. The mechanistic rationale is rank dependence: the mean rewards every positive uplift immediately; median and trimming rewards depend on where altered coordinates fall after sorting. Changing $\\alpha$ changes which ranks a coordinate can reach. It also changes each rule's attainable set. There is therefore no reason to interpret a difference computed only on currently successful targets as a pure cost difference without controlling the cohort. We assess response-dependent ordering directly, without attributing it to a new selection component.\n    75\t\n    76\tWithin any fixed rule and target, increasing $\\alpha$ cannot worsen attainability or increase the true minimum support: every coordinate and all three aggregators are monotone. This property does not constrain the sign of the difference between two rules' costs. A reversal of that difference is compatible with falling costs under both rules. Our primary contrast is the average median-minus-mean normalized support on targets attainable under both rules at both compared ceilings.\n    77\t\n    78\t\\subsection{Exact solutions, feasible witnesses, and bounds}\n    79\tThe frozen procedure begins by testing the full support. Since every allowed change is nonnegative and aggregation is coordinatewise monotone, failure of the full support certifies unattainability. This certificate is distinct from failure of a limited search. For a panel of at most six coordinates, supports are enumerated by cardinality, with deterministic semantic task-ID ordering. The first winning cardinality is exact. Thus every attainable primary six-task result used for the central contrast is a minimum-support result, not merely a successful heuristic plan.\n    80\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L99-L117]]\n    81\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n    82\t\n    83\tFor the mean on larger panels, sort headrooms $h_j=1-x_j$ from largest to smallest. Writing $h_{[j]}$ for this ordering, the minimum is the smallest $k$ satisfying\n    84\t\\begin{equation}\n    85\t \\frac{1}{m}\\sum_j x_j+\\frac{\\alpha}{m}\\sum_{j=1}^k h_{[j]}>b_{\\mathrm{mean}}+\\eps.\n    86\t \\label{eq:mean}\n    87\t\\end{equation}\n    88\tNo size-$k$ set can have greater summed headroom than this prefix, so a shorter failing prefix excludes all shorter supports. This explains exactness without invoking an unobserved solver behavior. Ties are resolved by semantic task identity.\n    89\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L118-L125]]\n    90\t\n    91\tFor larger median and trimmed panels, the procedure greedily adds the coordinate maximizing the immediate updated aggregate, again breaking ties by semantic task identity. It stops at a verified strict-first support. Its size is an upper bound $U$, not necessarily $\\cost_r$. Independently computed conservative lower bounds $L$ yield $L\\le\\cost_r\\le U$. The reported $L$ is the evaluator's verified reference bound, not a claim that the greedy procedure computes an exact optimum. Mean costs and exhaustive small-panel costs collapse to points. Unattainable cases have no finite support interval; the machine-readable out-of-range sentinel is never plotted as an investment cost.\n    92\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L126-L137]]\n    93\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L761-L789]]\n    94\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n    95\t\n    96\t\\subsection{Public score panels and experimental protocol}\n    97\tThe primary panels are HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 with 57 subject coordinates and the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates: IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro. The snapshots were acquired on 2026-11-17. The extracted records preserve original rows and settings; completeness and finite scores in the unit interval are required. These scores are not standardized effect sizes or observations from a homogeneous new evaluation. Source versions, checksums, filtering, and normalization are recorded in the supplementary reproduction specification.\n    98\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json]]\n    99\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/records.json]]\n   100\t\n   101\tModel aliases, normalized raw components, and score-map identities are grouped before partitioning the source records. The final evaluation uses a frozen, content-separated partition rather than a newly trained model. Within each family, rows tied for the lead under any studied rule are excluded from target selection. Remaining rows are sorted by mean score and divided into 24 bands; one target per band is sampled using each seed in $\\{1009,1013,1019\\}$. All rivals in the corresponding frozen pool remain visible. Each target is evaluated under every rule and ceiling, providing paired decisions rather than independently sampled rule-specific targets. Seeds vary target sampling, not model training.\n   102\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L614-L687]]\n   103\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#seeds]]\n   104\t\n   105\tThe evaluator checks returned supports against the supplied matrix and accepts exactness only when independently supported. The ordinary primary cases define the reported target attainment rate: a verified strict-first support with a supported claim, divided by all primary targets in that cell. Constant-ceiling, zero-response, joint column-permutation, and reset-replay checks test specific consistency properties. Replay carries no statistical weight. The completed frozen evaluation satisfies its recorded qualification requirements; this does not certify generalization outside the snapshots or validate unverified auxiliary diagnostics.\n   106\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json#protocol_policy]]\n   107\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n   108\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n   109\t\n   110\t\\subsection{Comparators, conditioning, and uncertainty}\n   111\tMatched selection comparators are no intervention, a seeded random coordinate order, and the marginal-aggregate greedy policy. These are policies on the same target, panel, rule, and ceiling, not component ablations. For policy comparisons, define penalized effort $E=|S|/m$ for a verified successful support and $E=1$ otherwise. Assigning one to nonattainment is a reporting convention, not an observed minimum cost. Support plots instead condition on joint attainability and show genuine finite bounds.\n   112\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L790-L818]]\n   113\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n   114\t\n   115\tThe central comparison keeps a fixed cohort per seed across both rules and both ceilings. The three-rule support plots use the within-ceiling joint cohort, which may differ across ceilings; seed means receive equal weight. We show individual seed summaries rather than inventing a population confidence interval. Existing policy intervals use 200 paired cluster resamples with resampling seed 48271 and percentile endpoints at 95\\%. They condition on the fixed snapshots and rival pools. They do not test differences between aggregation rules. Overlapping targets or related models across seeds further preclude treating these as independent training replications.\n   116\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1046-L1068]]\n   117\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#paired_intervals]]\n   118\t\n   119\t\\section{Results}\n   120\t\\subsection{Exact investment ordering changes with the response ceiling}\n   121\tOn the primary six-task panel, the fixed cohorts jointly attainable under mean and median at half and full headroom contain 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019, respectively. Figure~\\ref{fig:exact} shows the paired difference\n\n--- BIBLIOGRAPHY TITLES ---\n@article{MMLU,\n title={{Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding}},\n@article{BBH,\n title={{Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them}},\n@article{HELM,\n title={{Holistic Evaluation of Language Models}},\n@article{Lottery,\n title={{The Benchmark Lottery}},\n@article{SocialChoice,\n title={{Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory}},\n@article{Cheater,\n title={{Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater}},\n@article{Rephrased,\n title={{Rethinking Benchmark and Contamination for Language Models with Rephrased Samples}},\n@article{GPQA,\n title={{GPQA: A Graduate-Level Google-Proof Q\\&A Benchmark}},\n@article{IFEval,\n title={{Instruction-Following Evaluation for Large Language Models}},\n@article{MMLUPro,\n title={{MMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark}},\n@article{MATH,\n title={{Measuring Mathematical Problem Solving With the MATH Dataset}},\n@article{MuSR,\n title={{MuSR: Testing the Limits of Chain-of-thought with Multistep Soft Reasoning}},\n@article{BIGBench,\n title={{Beyond the Imitation Game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models}},\n@article{Datasheets,\n title={{Datasheets for Datasets}},\n@article{ModelCards,\n title={{Model Cards for Model Reporting}},\n@article{CheckList,\n title={{Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP models with CheckList}},\n@article{Underspecification,\n title={{Underspecification Presents Challenges for Credibility in Modern Machine Learning}},\n@article{ImageNetReplication,\n title={{Do ImageNet Classifiers Generalize to ImageNet?}},\n@article{TruthfulQA,\n title={{TruthfulQA: Measuring How Models Mimic Human Falsehoods}},\n@article{NumPy,\n title={{Array Programming with NumPy}},\n@article{SciPy,\n title={{SciPy 1.0--Fundamental Algorithms for Scientific Computing in Python}},\n@article{Demsar,\n title={{Statistical Comparisons of Classifiers over Multiple Data Sets}},\n@article{Cawley,\n title={{On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation}},\n@article{Huber,\n title={{Robust Estimation of a Location Parameter}},\n@article{Efron,\n title={{Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife}},\n\n--- PROVENANCE RECORD SAMPLE ---\n{\n  \"id\": \"MMLU\",\n  \"citation_key\": \"MMLU\",\n  \"title\": \"Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding\",\n  \"paper_id\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.03300\",\n  \"query\": \"Exact permitted canonical record: Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding\",\n  \"source_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.03300\",\n  \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.03300\",\n  \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n  \"evidence_path\": \"literature/MMLU.json\",\n  \"sha256\": \"03c49535bf302210a5a4ad028750906693c0b5e9340b1ffab9541e919d793f4c\",\n  \"reading_scope\": \"Canonical record metadata and abstract only; no citation graph or related links followed.\"\n}\n[\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Holistic Evaluation of Language Models\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: The Benchmark Lottery\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Rethinking Benchmark and Contamination for Language Models with Rephrased Samples\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: GPQA: A Graduate-Level Google-Proof Q&A Benchmark\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Instruction-Following Evaluation for Large Language Models\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: MMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Measuring Mathematical Problem Solving With the MATH Dataset\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: MuSR: Testing the Limits of Chain-of-thought with Multistep Soft Reasoning\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Beyond the Imitation Game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Datasheets for Datasets\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Model Cards for Model Reporting\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP models with CheckList\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Underspecification Presents Challenges for Credibility in Modern Machine Learning\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Do ImageNet Classifiers Generalize to ImageNet?\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: TruthfulQA: Measuring How Models Mimic Human Falsehoods\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Array Programming with NumPy\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: SciPy 1.0--Fundamental Algorithms for Scientific Computing in Python\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Statistical Comparisons of Classifiers over Multiple Data Sets\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Robust Estimation of a Location Parameter\",\n  \"Exact permitted canonical record: Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife\"\n]\n\n--- SUMMARY RESTRICTIONS AND SOURCES ---\npaper_strategy.json:7:  \"narrative_rationale\": \"This is a bounded counterfactual empirical study, not a new optimizer or training study. Existing final decision records support a direct rule-by-response comparison, including an exact matched-cohort reversal on the six-task Open LLM panel and explicit uncertainty about optimal support on the 57-subject HELM panel. The same scientific question organizes these as complementary evidence blocks with different certificate strength, not as transfer or discovery-order experiments. The frozen reference procedure is the studied method; unsuccessful search proposals are not the contribution. No universal ordering, practical training cost, or causal contamination effect is inferred.\",\npaper_strategy.json:16:    \"The supplied bibliography contains metadata retrieval records, many tangential to this study, without evidence of substantive reading. Its presence does not license scientific positioning or citation padding. No external source was accessed during strategy generation.\"\npaper_strategy.json:46:    \"Primary thesis test: ordinary Open LLM primary decisions only; match by seed and target slot and retain targets attainable under both mean and median at alpha=0.5 and alpha=1.0. Compare exact support fractions on that identical per-seed cohort across both response conditions. The recorded data give a negative median-minus-mean average at half headroom and a positive average at full headroom for every final seed. This is a descriptive within-snapshot reversal, not a pre-registered significance claim.\",\npaper_strategy.json:47:    \"Report attainability for all sampled ordinary primary targets at alpha=0.25, 0.5, and 1.0, separately for HELM and Open LLM, so conditioning the exact contrast on attainable cases cannot conceal infeasible targets.\",\npaper_strategy.json:49:    \"Retain matched policy comparisons, preferably a compact main table at alpha=0.5 and the complete grid after references. State where exact selection reduces support relative to greedy and where it ties. Existing paired intervals compare the selected procedure against random or greedy; they are not cross-rule confidence intervals.\",\npaper_strategy.json:55:    \"Figure 2, central exact comparison: two matched-cohort dot plots, one for half headroom and one for full headroom, showing median-minus-mean minimum-support fraction by final sampling seed with a zero reference line.\",\npaper_strategy.json:57:    \"Use tables, not additional main figures, for snapshot scope, comparator differences, and certificate/status counts. Put the full conditional comparator-interval grid and auxiliary sensitivities after references.\"\npaper_strategy.json:83:      \"purpose\": \"Display the exact within-snapshot mean–median ordering reversal without infeasibility penalties, composition changes across response conditions, or search chronology.\",\npaper_strategy.json:88:      \"split_by\": \"alpha=0.5 versus alpha=1.0 on the ordinary Open LLM primary panel\",\npaper_strategy.json:98:      \"content_constraints\": \"Filter ordinary primary Open LLM decisions, join rule/alpha cells by seed and slot, and intersect attainability under both rules at both response conditions. Each seed retains the same cohort in both panels. Plot exact support-count differences divided by six; show individual seed summaries, not invented confidence intervals or a pooled population p-value.\"\npaper_strategy.json:108:      \"split_by\": \"HELM 57-subject panel versus Open LLM six-task panel\",\npaper_strategy.json:122:    \"purpose\": \"Provide substantive mathematical, conditional-uncertainty, sensitivity, and reproducibility depth after references without hiding the central exact contrast, unattainability, or larger-panel certification limits from the main body.\",\npaper_strategy.json:131:        \"grounded_content\": \"Tabulate ordinary primary decisions by family, seed, rule, and ceiling, retaining attainment counts, exact/interval status, lower bounds, feasible support sizes, and all zero/random/greedy references. Include the already-recorded per-seed cluster-percentile intervals for selected-minus-random and selected-minus-greedy effort, their conditional scope and all null effects; do not reinterpret them as cross-rule inference.\",\npaper_strategy.json:151:        \"grounded_content\": \"Use existing source records and manifest to document HELM release, the two pinned Open LLM revisions, acquisition time, source and extracted-record hashes, normalization, alias grouping, filtering, target strata, seeds, dependency versions, and CPU commands. Map every eventual plot/table to its derived-data script and source slice; mark those scripts as a writer-stage deliverable, not already present. State unresolved score-data redistribution licensing and variable evaluation settings.\",\npaper_strategy.json:156:        \"grounded_content\": \"Disclose the documented LLM role in research ideation, code preparation, reference-query planning, and manuscript framing; add writing/figure assistance only when actually used. State that counterfactual score edits are not model training. Do not invent model identities, provider details, human validation, or a literature-exposure history.\",\npaper_strategy.json:169:    \"Body plan: aim for about 8.5 substantive pages, excluding references: 1.25 pages for abstract/introduction and contribution scope, 0.75 for safely grounded related work and modeling context, 1.0 for objective/assumptions, 1.0 for solver and certificate scope, 0.75 for experimental protocol and uncertainty, 2.75 for exact contrast/attainability/support/comparators including figures, and 1.0 for limitations and conclusion. Figure area is included, not added on top. An eight-page body must fill at least 75 percent of its last page; never pad to reach this threshold.\",\npaper_strategy.json:178:    \"Uncertainty contract: final sampling seeds reuse fixed public panels and may share targets or model ancestry; they are not independent training replicates. Existing paired intervals use 200 cluster resamples with RNG seed 48271 and are conditional on fixed snapshots/rival pools. Keep all three seed summaries visible or tabulated. No existing cross-rule interval or p-value supports the reversal, and baseline intervals cannot be reassigned to it.\",\npaper_strategy.json:179:    \"Main tables: a compact scope table distinguishing public source snapshots from the evaluated extracted panels; an investment/certificate table with cohort sizes and infeasible counts; and a matched policy-difference table at alpha=0.5 for both families and all three rules. Include ties with greedy; put the complete already-recorded interval grid after references. These are planned transformations of existing records, not unperformed experiments.\",\npaper_strategy.json:180:    \"The main body must retain the exact six-task comparison, unconditional attainment rates, conditioning definitions, larger-panel interval caveat, uncertainty scope, and failed-search disclosure in brief. Supplementary material expands them; it must not hide contradictory or essential evidence. Route any separately supplied authoritative appendix-ranked analyses after references without inventing a ranking or retroactively changing this strategy.\",\npaper_strategy.json:181:    \"Provenance contract: HELM v1.13.0; Open LLM contents revision 9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2; separate results revision aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5. The final manifest records acquisition on 2026-11-17 and extracted records SHA-256 d80abb1b13c6962e15f04d9794fc18fcf39e91cf7c79c679e233d3031f934398. Use task/resources/final/manifest.json and records.json for source/normalization/license details. Do not claim evaluation of every row in the public repositories.\",\npaper_strategy.json:182:    \"BBH scope: the existing manifest records model-revision reconciliation but explicitly says contents and results are distinct runs with potentially different settings. Auxiliary Open LLM odd slots route to BBH and even slots to the primary-family projection. Use the routing code to separate them, not the generic auxiliary label alone. BBH is not an independent replicated transfer benchmark.\",\npaper_strategy.json:183:    \"Literature contract: cite only existing bibliography keys after safely reading the actual permitted source; retrieval metadata is not substantive reading. Many supplied entries are tangential, and several relevant starter records are absent, so bibliography curation and safe substantive reading remain writer-stage prerequisites before related-work claims. Do not fill space with unrelated citations or claim novelty against unread papers. Never search for the prohibited study, expand citation graphs, or infer its identity; log and stop any accidental exposure.\",\npaper_strategy.json:185:    \"Keep artifact and internal decision terminology private. Reader-facing terms are target attainment rate, support fraction, strict first place, exact minimum support, certified infeasibility, and feasible support interval. Do not turn the selected reference into a novel learned method, or experimental controls into scientific success rates.\",\npaper_strategy.json:186:    \"Required separate LLM Usage disclosure belongs after references if needed; remove names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements, and identifying repository links. Describe only actual assistance documented in run artifacts. Do not claim human checks, literature reading, or training that did not occur.\",\npaper_strategy.json:187:    \"This strategy creates neither a manuscript nor claim-mapping artifacts, generates no figures, performs no new research experiment, and does not run final-paper validation. A ready framing is not a claim that manuscript reproduction scripts, substantive citation verification, or a compiled paper already exist.\"\n\n--- MEMORY HELPER ---\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Append validated local/global memory entries for idea workflows.\n\nTwo modes:\n\n* default (event append) — appends one memory event to ``memory.md`` (local +\n  global) plus one JSONL record to ``memory.jsonl``. Existing behavior.\n* ``--write-conclusions`` — atomically replaces ``conclusions.md`` with a\n  validated single-paragraph distilled summary and appends a record to\n  ``conclusions_history.jsonl``. Validation rejects empty input,\n  multi-paragraph input, or input over 300 words.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport fcntl\nimport json\nimport os\nimport stat\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any\n\nCONCLUSIONS_WORD_LIMIT = 300\nGATE_TRIGGER_VALUES = (\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\")\n\nLOCAL_MEMORY_HEADERS = \"\"\"# Memory Log\n\n## Context\n## Idea Discoveries\n## Program Evaluations\n## Failures / Regressions\n## Next Hypotheses\n\"\"\"\n\nGLOBAL_MEMORY_HEADER = \"# Global Memory Log\\n\\n\"\n\n\ndef utc_now() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the current UTC timestamp string.\"\"\"\n    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()\n\n\ndef _load_event(*, event_json: str | None, event_file: Path | None) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Load event.\"\"\"\n    if event_json:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_json)\n    elif event_file is not None:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_file.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n    else:\n        raw = input().strip()\n        if not raw:\n            raise ValueError(\"missing event payload\")\n        parsed = json.loads(raw)\n    if not isinstance(parsed, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"event payload must be a JSON object\")\n    return parsed\n\n\ndef _ensure_required_fields(event: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Ensure required fields.\"\"\"\n    normalized = dict(event)\n    if not isinstance(normalized.get(\"timestamp\"), str) or not normalized[\"timestamp\"].strip():\n        normalized[\"timestamp\"] = utc_now()\n\n    required_string_fields = (\"step\", \"scope\", \"summary\", \"evidence\", \"next_action\")\n    for key in required_string_fields:\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing required string field: {key}\")\n        normalized[key] = value.strip()\n\n    for key in (\"idea_id\", \"attempt_id\", \"program_id\"):\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if value is None:\n            continue\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            normalized[key] = str(value)\n        normalized[key] = normalized[key].strip() or None\n\n    metrics = normalized.get(\"metrics\")\n    if metrics is not None and not isinstance(metrics, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"metrics must be an object when present\")\n    rubrics = normalized.get(\"rubrics\")\n    if rubrics is not None and not isinstance(rubrics, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"rubrics must be an array when present\")\n    tags = normalized.get(\"tags\")\n    if tags is not None:\n        if not isinstance(tags, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"tags must be an array when present\")\n        normalized[\"tags\"] = [str(item).strip() for item in tags if str(item).strip()]\n    return normalized\n\n\ndef _markdown_entry(event: dict[str, Any]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Handle markdown entry logic for this module.\"\"\"\n    lines = [\n        f\"### {event['timestamp']} | step={event['step']} | scope={event['scope']}\",\n        f\"- summary: {event['summary']}\",\n        f\"- evidence: {event['evidence']}\",\n        f\"- next_action: {event['next_action']}\",\n    ]\n\n    for key in (\"idea_id\", \"attempt_id\", \"program_id\"):\n        value = event.get(key)\n        if isinstance(value, str) and value.strip():\n            lines.append(f\"- {key}: {value.strip()}\")\n\n    metrics = event.get(\"metrics\")\n    if isinstance(metrics, dict) and metrics:\n        lines.append(f\"- metrics: {json.dumps(metrics, sort_keys=True)}\")\n    rubrics = event.get(\"rubrics\")\n    if isinstance(rubrics, list) and rubrics:\n        lines.append(f\"- rubrics: {json.dumps(rubrics, sort_keys=True)}\")\n    tags = event.get(\"tags\")\n    if isinstance(tags, list) and tags:\n        lines.append(f\"- tags: {', '.join(str(item) for item in tags)}\")\n\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\\n\"\n\n\ndef _append_text(path: Path, text: str, *, header: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Safely initialize and append text to a regular file.\"\"\"\n    fd = _open_regular_for_append(path)\n    try:\n        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)\n        size = os.fstat(fd).st_size\n        if not size:\n            _write_all(fd, header.encode(\"utf-8\"))\n        elif os.pread(fd, 1, size - 1) != b\"\\n\":\n            _write_all(fd, b\"\\n\")\n        _write_all(fd, text.encode(\"utf-8\"))\n    finally:\n        try:\n            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n        finally:\n            os.close(fd)\n\n\ndef _append_jsonl(path: Path, payload: dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Append one JSON object as a JSONL record.\"\"\"\n    line = (json.dumps(payload, sort_keys=True) + \"\\n\").encode(\"utf-8\")\n    fd = _open_regular_for_append(path)\n    try:\n        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)\n        size = os.fstat(fd).st_size\n        if size and os.pread(fd, 1, size - 1) != b\"\\n\":\n            _write_all(fd, b\"\\n\")\n        _write_all(fd, line)\n    finally:\n        try:\n            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n        finally:\n            os.close(fd)\n\n\ndef _open_regular_for_append(path: Path) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Open an append target without following its parent or leaf.\"\"\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"reasoning","text":"**Reducing output size**"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- SCHEMA KEYS ---\\\\n'; jq 'keys, .constraints, .failure_reason' reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- METHOD TARGET ---\\\\n'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '75,115p'; printf '\\\\n--- BIBLIOGRAPHY TITLES ---\\\\n'; rg '\"'^@| title='\"' references.bib; printf '\\\\n--- PROVENANCE COVERAGE ---\\\\n'; jq '{count, references: [.references[] | {citation_key, title, source, evidence_level}]}' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- STRATEGY KEYS ---\\\\n'; jq 'keys' paper_strategy.json; printf '\\\\n--- SUMMARY OPENING ---\\\\n'; sed -n '1,55p' global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- SCHEMA KEYS ---\\\\n'; jq 'keys, .constraints, .failure_reason' reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- METHOD TARGET ---\\\\n'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '75,115p'; printf '\\\\n--- BIBLIOGRAPHY TITLES ---\\\\n'; rg '\"'^@| title='\"' references.bib; printf '\\\\n--- PROVENANCE COVERAGE ---\\\\n'; jq '{count, references: [.references[] | {citation_key, title, source, evidence_level}]}' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- STRATEGY KEYS ---\\\\n'; jq 'keys' paper_strategy.json; printf '\\\\n--- SUMMARY OPENING ---\\\\n'; sed -n '1,55p' global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n--- SCHEMA KEYS ---\n[\n  \"architectural_shifts\",\n  \"benchmarks\",\n  \"central_topic\",\n  \"comparators\",\n  \"core_methods\",\n  \"failure_reason\",\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\",\n  \"queries\",\n  \"result_driven_themes\",\n  \"status\"\n]\nnull\n\"\"\n\n--- METHOD TARGET ---\n    75\t\n    76\tWithin any fixed rule and target, increasing $\\alpha$ cannot worsen attainability or increase the true minimum support: every coordinate and all three aggregators are monotone. This property does not constrain the sign of the difference between two rules' costs. A reversal of that difference is compatible with falling costs under both rules. Our primary contrast is the average median-minus-mean normalized support on targets attainable under both rules at both compared ceilings.\n    77\t\n    78\t\\subsection{Exact solutions, feasible witnesses, and bounds}\n    79\tThe frozen procedure begins by testing the full support. Since every allowed change is nonnegative and aggregation is coordinatewise monotone, failure of the full support certifies unattainability. This certificate is distinct from failure of a limited search. For a panel of at most six coordinates, supports are enumerated by cardinality, with deterministic semantic task-ID ordering. The first winning cardinality is exact. Thus every attainable primary six-task result used for the central contrast is a minimum-support result, not merely a successful heuristic plan.\n    80\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L99-L117]]\n    81\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n    82\t\n    83\tFor the mean on larger panels, sort headrooms $h_j=1-x_j$ from largest to smallest. Writing $h_{[j]}$ for this ordering, the minimum is the smallest $k$ satisfying\n    84\t\\begin{equation}\n    85\t \\frac{1}{m}\\sum_j x_j+\\frac{\\alpha}{m}\\sum_{j=1}^k h_{[j]}>b_{\\mathrm{mean}}+\\eps.\n    86\t \\label{eq:mean}\n    87\t\\end{equation}\n    88\tNo size-$k$ set can have greater summed headroom than this prefix, so a shorter failing prefix excludes all shorter supports. This explains exactness without invoking an unobserved solver behavior. Ties are resolved by semantic task identity.\n    89\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L118-L125]]\n    90\t\n    91\tFor larger median and trimmed panels, the procedure greedily adds the coordinate maximizing the immediate updated aggregate, again breaking ties by semantic task identity. It stops at a verified strict-first support. Its size is an upper bound $U$, not necessarily $\\cost_r$. Independently computed conservative lower bounds $L$ yield $L\\le\\cost_r\\le U$. The reported $L$ is the evaluator's verified reference bound, not a claim that the greedy procedure computes an exact optimum. Mean costs and exhaustive small-panel costs collapse to points. Unattainable cases have no finite support interval; the machine-readable out-of-range sentinel is never plotted as an investment cost.\n    92\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L126-L137]]\n    93\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L761-L789]]\n    94\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n    95\t\n    96\t\\subsection{Public score panels and experimental protocol}\n    97\tThe primary panels are HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 with 57 subject coordinates and the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates: IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro. The snapshots were acquired on 2026-11-17. The extracted records preserve original rows and settings; completeness and finite scores in the unit interval are required. These scores are not standardized effect sizes or observations from a homogeneous new evaluation. Source versions, checksums, filtering, and normalization are recorded in the supplementary reproduction specification.\n    98\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json]]\n    99\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/records.json]]\n   100\t\n   101\tModel aliases, normalized raw components, and score-map identities are grouped before partitioning the source records. The final evaluation uses a frozen, content-separated partition rather than a newly trained model. Within each family, rows tied for the lead under any studied rule are excluded from target selection. Remaining rows are sorted by mean score and divided into 24 bands; one target per band is sampled using each seed in $\\{1009,1013,1019\\}$. All rivals in the corresponding frozen pool remain visible. Each target is evaluated under every rule and ceiling, providing paired decisions rather than independently sampled rule-specific targets. Seeds vary target sampling, not model training.\n   102\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L614-L687]]\n   103\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#seeds]]\n   104\t\n   105\tThe evaluator checks returned supports against the supplied matrix and accepts exactness only when independently supported. The ordinary primary cases define the reported target attainment rate: a verified strict-first support with a supported claim, divided by all primary targets in that cell. Constant-ceiling, zero-response, joint column-permutation, and reset-replay checks test specific consistency properties. Replay carries no statistical weight. The completed frozen evaluation satisfies its recorded qualification requirements; this does not certify generalization outside the snapshots or validate unverified auxiliary diagnostics.\n   106\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json#protocol_policy]]\n   107\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n   108\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n   109\t\n   110\t\\subsection{Comparators, conditioning, and uncertainty}\n   111\tMatched selection comparators are no intervention, a seeded random coordinate order, and the marginal-aggregate greedy policy. These are policies on the same target, panel, rule, and ceiling, not component ablations. For policy comparisons, define penalized effort $E=|S|/m$ for a verified successful support and $E=1$ otherwise. Assigning one to nonattainment is a reporting convention, not an observed minimum cost. Support plots instead condition on joint attainability and show genuine finite bounds.\n   112\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L790-L818]]\n   113\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n   114\t\n   115\tThe central comparison keeps a fixed cohort per seed across both rules and both ceilings. The three-rule support plots use the within-ceiling joint cohort, which may differ across ceilings; seed means receive equal weight. We show individual seed summaries rather than inventing a population confidence interval. Existing policy intervals use 200 paired cluster resamples with resampling seed 48271 and percentile endpoints at 95\\%. They condition on the fixed snapshots and rival pools. They do not test differences between aggregation rules. Overlapping targets or related models across seeds further preclude treating these as independent training replications.\n\n--- BIBLIOGRAPHY TITLES ---\n@article{MMLU,\n title={{Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding}},\n@article{BBH,\n title={{Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them}},\n@article{HELM,\n title={{Holistic Evaluation of Language Models}},\n@article{Lottery,\n title={{The Benchmark Lottery}},\n@article{SocialChoice,\n title={{Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory}},\n@article{Cheater,\n title={{Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater}},\n@article{Rephrased,\n title={{Rethinking Benchmark and Contamination for Language Models with Rephrased Samples}},\n@article{GPQA,\n title={{GPQA: A Graduate-Level Google-Proof Q\\&A Benchmark}},\n@article{IFEval,\n title={{Instruction-Following Evaluation for Large Language Models}},\n@article{MMLUPro,\n title={{MMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark}},\n@article{MATH,\n title={{Measuring Mathematical Problem Solving With the MATH Dataset}},\n@article{MuSR,\n title={{MuSR: Testing the Limits of Chain-of-thought with Multistep Soft Reasoning}},\n@article{BIGBench,\n title={{Beyond the Imitation Game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models}},\n@article{Datasheets,\n title={{Datasheets for Datasets}},\n@article{ModelCards,\n title={{Model Cards for Model Reporting}},\n@article{CheckList,\n title={{Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP models with CheckList}},\n@article{Underspecification,\n title={{Underspecification Presents Challenges for Credibility in Modern Machine Learning}},\n@article{ImageNetReplication,\n title={{Do ImageNet Classifiers Generalize to ImageNet?}},\n@article{TruthfulQA,\n title={{TruthfulQA: Measuring How Models Mimic Human Falsehoods}},\n@article{NumPy,\n title={{Array Programming with NumPy}},\n@article{SciPy,\n title={{SciPy 1.0--Fundamental Algorithms for Scientific Computing in Python}},\n@article{Demsar,\n title={{Statistical Comparisons of Classifiers over Multiple Data Sets}},\n@article{Cawley,\n title={{On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation}},\n@article{Huber,\n title={{Robust Estimation of a Location Parameter}},\n@article{Efron,\n title={{Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife}},\n\n--- PROVENANCE COVERAGE ---\n{\n  \"count\": 25,\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"MMLU\",\n      \"title\": \"Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"BBH\",\n      \"title\": \"Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"HELM\",\n      \"title\": \"Holistic Evaluation of Language Models\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"Lottery\",\n      \"title\": \"The Benchmark Lottery\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"SocialChoice\",\n      \"title\": \"Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"Cheater\",\n      \"title\": \"Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"Rephrased\",\n      \"title\": \"Rethinking Benchmark and Contamination for Language Models with Rephrased Samples\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"GPQA\",\n      \"title\": \"GPQA: A Graduate-Level Google-Proof Q&A Benchmark\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"IFEval\",\n      \"title\": \"Instruction-Following Evaluation for Large Language Models\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"MMLUPro\",\n      \"title\": \"MMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"MATH\",\n      \"title\": \"Measuring Mathematical Problem Solving With the MATH Dataset\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"MuSR\",\n      \"title\": \"MuSR: Testing the Limits of Chain-of-thought with Multistep Soft Reasoning\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"BIGBench\",\n      \"title\": \"Beyond the Imitation Game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"Datasheets\",\n      \"title\": \"Datasheets for Datasets\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"ModelCards\",\n      \"title\": \"Model Cards for Model Reporting\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"CheckList\",\n      \"title\": \"Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP models with CheckList\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"Underspecification\",\n      \"title\": \"Underspecification Presents Challenges for Credibility in Modern Machine Learning\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"ImageNetReplication\",\n      \"title\": \"Do ImageNet Classifiers Generalize to ImageNet?\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"TruthfulQA\",\n      \"title\": \"TruthfulQA: Measuring How Models Mimic Human Falsehoods\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"NumPy\",\n      \"title\": \"Array Programming with NumPy\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"SciPy\",\n      \"title\": \"SciPy 1.0--Fundamental Algorithms for Scientific Computing in Python\",\n      \"source\": \"arxiv\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"Demsar\",\n      \"title\": \"Statistical Comparisons of Classifiers over Multiple Data Sets\",\n      \"source\": \"publisher\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"Cawley\",\n      \"title\": \"On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation\",\n      \"source\": \"publisher\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"Huber\",\n      \"title\": \"Robust Estimation of a Location Parameter\",\n      \"source\": \"publisher\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"citation_key\": \"Efron\",\n      \"title\": \"Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife\",\n      \"source\": \"publisher\",\n      \"evidence_level\": null\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n--- STRATEGY KEYS ---\n[\n  \"central_question\",\n  \"central_thesis\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\",\n  \"failure_reason\",\n  \"figure_plan\",\n  \"figure_specs\",\n  \"forbidden_headings\",\n  \"forbidden_phrases\",\n  \"method_groups\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\",\n  \"notes_for_writer\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\",\n  \"recommended_title\",\n  \"results_focus\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\",\n  \"status\",\n  \"supplementary_plan\"\n]\n\n--- SUMMARY OPENING ---\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected Certified Attainability Frontiers for Targeted Leaderboard Investment; wrote matching idea and rubric bundles. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Only an untested proposal and the supplied incumbent aggregate are available, so no evidence-backed distilled recommendation is added.\n  - evidence: Supplied bootstrap correct=true and combined_score=0.53012102014295; no recent ideas or attempts. Named external incumbent source was unavailable; implementation details and failure causes remain unknown. Alternatives were perfect-score-only attacks, unsupported training-cost curves, and a wider aggregation-rule catalogue. Bounded score-response ceilings permit exact support-cost certificates using documented public score matrices. No experiments or literature searches were run. Only canonical local memory path ideas/01_certified_attainability_frontiers_for_targeted_leaderboard_investment/memory.md was created for this idea.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T12:50:05.435064+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.42832805393112416, correct=False; scientific acceptance failed; cause unavailable; correct=false alone does not establish a protocol or implementation defect; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.42832805393112416, \"control_pass_rate\": 0.9525462962962963, \"correct\": false, \"feasible_rate\": 0.4027777777777778, \"mean_effort_fraction\": 0.7963369070825209, \"mean_verified_gap_fraction\": 0.18981481481481483}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected Amortized Rank Window Certificates for Targeted Investment and wrote matching bundles. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=The proposal is untested, the recorded failure cause remains unavailable, and interface restrictions were already recorded rather than newly established scientific findings.\n  - evidence: Read named task/results/memory/skills and prior candidate source/spec. Interface supports uploaded matrices, mean/median/trimmed, common alpha, strict-first margin 1e-10, original-column outputs, work_seconds and node_budget. Prior source repeats whole-matrix aggregation per decision; amortization is a hypothesis, not a failure explanation. Prior combined_score=0.42832805393112416, correct=false, control_pass_rate=0.9525462962962963, feasible_rate=0.4027777777777778, mean_effort_fraction=0.7963369070825209, mean_verified_gap_fraction=0.18981481481481483; incumbent combined_score=0.53012102014295, correct=true. External incumbent source unavailable. Alternatives: repeated prefix-only certification, unsupported Borda/paper operations, and unobserved training costs. Selected conservative rank-window envelopes plus verified supports and shared preprocessing. No project code, tests, evaluator, literature search, or computational probes run. Canonical local target computed once after ID/title finalization: ideas/02_amortized_rank_window_certificates_for_targeted_investment/memory.md; no alternate same-cycle target created or used.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T13:06:05.197872+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.42913823911630933, correct=False; scientific acceptance failed; cause unavailable; correct=false alone does not establish a protocol or implementation defect; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.429138239